"use strict";(self.webpackChunkhalt_website=self.webpackChunkhalt_website||[]).push([["36607"],{77735(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"pocket-opossum-discovery","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/pocket-opossum-discovery","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-01-pocket-opossum/index.md","title":"HALT Announces Discovery of New Species: Microdidelphis domesticus (\\"Pocket Opossum\\")","description":"A small animal with an opossum face and hamster-sized body, sitting in soft bedding","date":"2026-04-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"News","permalink":"/blog/tags/news","description":"News and announcements"},{"inline":false,"label":"Hamsters","permalink":"/blog/tags/hamsters","description":"Hamster updates and stories"},{"inline":true,"label":"aprilfools","permalink":"/blog/tags/aprilfools"}],"readingTime":0.9,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"pocket-opossum-discovery","title":"HALT Announces Discovery of New Species: Microdidelphis domesticus (\\"Pocket Opossum\\")","authors":["danni"],"tags":["news","hamsters","aprilfools"],"image":"./pocket-opossum.jpeg","date":"2026-04-01T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Ilya\'s Redemption Arc: When Neutering Is the Answer","permalink":"/blog/ilya-neuter-redemption-arc"}},"content":"![A small animal with an opossum face and hamster-sized body, sitting in soft bedding](./pocket-opossum.jpeg)\\n\\nHelping All Little Things (HALT) has announced the discovery of *Microdidelphis domesticus*, or the **\\"Pocket Opossum,\\"** identified during a recent intake by:\\n\\n- **Danni**, President & Small Animal Specialist\\n- **Jen**, Board Member & Even Smaller Animal Specialist\\n\\nThe animal was initially logged as *\\"hamster (?)\\"*\u2026 but quickly raised concerns.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n\\"The longer we looked, the clearer it became \u2014 this is not a hamster,\\" said Danni.\\n\\n\\"We ran extensive tests: snack trials, habitat observations, and a lot of staring,\\" added Jen. \\"Conclusion: opossum. But tiny.\\"\\n\\n## Key Traits\\n\\n- Opossum face, hamster-sized body\\n- Small, deliberate movements (wise? confused?)\\n- Strong preference for snacks and soft bedding\\n- Causes immediate existential questioning\\n- Vibe: small, but significant\\n\\nResearchers are split between *\\"new micro-marsupial\\"* and *\\"nature just doing something weird again.\\"*\\n\\nHALT has classified the species as **\\"Extremely Small\\"** and recommends care involving snacks, admiration, and gentle reassurance.\\n\\nSightings of suspiciously tiny opossums should be reported immediately.\\n\\n---\\n\\n*Note: This is an April Fool\'s release. The animal is real. The science\u2026 pending.*\\n\\n*#AprilFools*\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"ilya-neuter-redemption-arc","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/ilya-neuter-redemption-arc","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-28-ilya-neuter/2026-03-28-ilya-neuter.md","title":"Ilya\'s Redemption Arc: When Neutering Is the Answer","description":"Ilya is not happy about recent events. His cagemates and foster parents, however, are absolutely thrilled.","date":"2026-03-28T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Rats","permalink":"/blog/tags/rats","description":"Rat updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"},{"inline":false,"label":"Educational","permalink":"/blog/tags/educational","description":"Educational content"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"}],"readingTime":1.44,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"ilya-neuter-redemption-arc","title":"Ilya\'s Redemption Arc: When Neutering Is the Answer","date":"2026-03-28T00:00:00.000Z","authors":["danni"],"tags":["rats","medical","educational","sanctuary"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"HALT Announces Discovery of New Species: Microdidelphis domesticus (\\"Pocket Opossum\\")","permalink":"/blog/pocket-opossum-discovery"},"nextItem":{"title":"Matilda\'s Arthritis and the Start of Adequan Trials for Our Special Needs Herd","permalink":"/blog/matilda-arthritis-adequan-update"}},"content":"Ilya is not happy about recent events. His cagemates and foster parents, however, are absolutely thrilled.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nIlya came to us from the Long Island rat rescue earlier this year \u2014 one of 32 rats taken in from a difficult situation. He is a silly, brave, curious, and food-motivated boy who also happens to have developed a significant aggression problem. He has been biting his friends and caretakers, which is not something we can allow to continue \u2014 both for the safety of everyone around him and for his own quality of life.\\n\\nFor most male rats, neutering is never necessary. They live full, happy lives intact. But occasionally, a male rat will develop hormone-driven aggression that makes him a danger to his companions and difficult to handle safely. In those cases, neutering is typically the most effective solution.\\n\\nIlya had his procedure, and now begins the waiting game. Over the next two to four weeks, we should start to see improvement as his testosterone levels drop. Full dropoff takes about eight weeks. The hope is that the Ilya who emerges on the other side is the same silly, food-motivated, curious boy \u2014 just without the biting.\\n\\nEveryone wish Ilya a smooth recovery and a wonderful redemption arc. \u{1F400}\\n\\n:::info Neutering Male Rats\\nMost male rats do not need to be neutered. However, neutering can be beneficial in cases of hormone-driven aggression, certain tumors, or when bonding with a female. It is a low-risk procedure when performed by an experienced exotic vet. If your male rat is showing aggression, consult your vet before assuming neutering is necessary \u2014 behavioral causes should be ruled out first.\\n\\n[Read our Rat Care Guide \u2192](/docs/Rats/getting-started/rat-care-guide)\\n:::"},{"id":"matilda-arthritis-adequan-update","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/matilda-arthritis-adequan-update","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-28-matilda-arthritis/2026-03-28-matilda-arthritis.md","title":"Matilda\'s Arthritis and the Start of Adequan Trials for Our Special Needs Herd","description":"Matilda\'s arthritis has progressed significantly. The severity in her knee is now affecting the way she walks \u2014 more noticeably than her already distinctive gait. We are increasing her pain management and, importantly, beginning Adequan trials for the guinea pigs in our sanctuary with the most advanced cases of osteodystrophy and arthritis.","date":"2026-03-28T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"},{"inline":false,"label":"Educational","permalink":"/blog/tags/educational","description":"Educational content"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"}],"readingTime":1.48,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"matilda-arthritis-adequan-update","title":"Matilda\'s Arthritis and the Start of Adequan Trials for Our Special Needs Herd","date":"2026-03-28T00:00:00.000Z","authors":["danni"],"tags":["guineapigs","medical","educational","sanctuary"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Ilya\'s Redemption Arc: When Neutering Is the Answer","permalink":"/blog/ilya-neuter-redemption-arc"},"nextItem":{"title":"Good News for Story: Just an Infection, Not Something Worse","permalink":"/blog/story-good-news"}},"content":"Matilda\'s arthritis has progressed significantly. The severity in her knee is now affecting the way she walks \u2014 more noticeably than her already distinctive gait. We are increasing her pain management and, importantly, beginning **Adequan trials** for the guinea pigs in our sanctuary with the most advanced cases of osteodystrophy and arthritis.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![Matilda resting comfortably](./photo-01.jpg)\\n*Matilda, resting and receiving extra care as we adjust her pain management protocol.*\\n\\nFor those who have been following along: we ran an Adequan trial with Tally earlier this year, which showed promising results. Based on that experience, we are now expanding the trial to other pigs in the sanctuary who are dealing with advanced joint disease \u2014 the ones for whom the active, busy herd environment has become harder to navigate.\\n\\nOne of the immediate goals is to set up a dedicated 2\xd76 space for the pigs with advanced mobility issues. A calmer, more accessible environment will make a real difference in their daily quality of life while we work on the medical side.\\n\\nMatilda is a fighter and has always adapted to whatever challenges come her way. We are hopeful that the combination of adjusted pain management and Adequan will give her more comfortable, mobile days ahead.\\n\\n:::info About Adequan for Guinea Pigs\\nAdequan (polysulfated glycosaminoglycan) is an injectable medication typically used in dogs and horses for joint disease, but it has been used off-label in guinea pigs with promising results for managing arthritis and osteodystrophy. It works by supporting cartilage health and reducing inflammation in the joints. If your guinea pig is showing signs of joint pain or mobility issues, discuss all options with your exotic vet.\\n\\n[Read about spine and musculoskeletal issues in guinea pigs \u2192](/docs/guinea-pigs/illnesses-and-conditions/spine-issues)\\n:::"},{"id":"story-good-news","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/story-good-news","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-28-story-update/2026-03-28-story-update.md","title":"Good News for Story: Just an Infection, Not Something Worse","description":"After a worrying week, we have good news: Story\'s lumps are not what we feared.","date":"2026-03-28T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"}],"readingTime":1.13,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"story-good-news","title":"Good News for Story: Just an Infection, Not Something Worse","date":"2026-03-28T00:00:00.000Z","authors":["danni"],"tags":["guineapigs","medical","sanctuary"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Matilda\'s Arthritis and the Start of Adequan Trials for Our Special Needs Herd","permalink":"/blog/matilda-arthritis-adequan-update"},"nextItem":{"title":"One Year with Sonnet: A Rare Reproductive Condition and a Remarkable Recovery","permalink":"/blog/sonnet-one-year-ovarian-cysts"}},"content":"After a worrying week, we have good news: Story\'s lumps are not what we feared.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![Story at his vet appointment, looking adorable](./photo-01.jpg)\\n*Story being the absolute goodest boy at his appointment \u2014 even Dr. Ford wanted to take him home.*\\n\\nStory had a couple of suspicious lumps that needed investigating, and we were understandably anxious heading into his appointment at Southern Maine Veterinary. But Story was the goodest boy throughout the whole visit, and the news was a relief: he has a small infection that triggered a lymph node to swell. After a course of antibiotics, he should be completely back to normal.\\n\\nDr. Ford was so charmed by Story that he reportedly wanted to take him home. We understand completely.\\n\\nStory will be on antibiotics for the next little while, and we will keep everyone updated on his progress. For now, he is comfortable, eating well, and being his usual photogenic self.\\n\\n:::info Lumps in Guinea Pigs\\nNot every lump in a guinea pig is cause for alarm \u2014 lymph node swelling from infections is common and very treatable. However, lumps should always be evaluated by an exotic vet, as they can also indicate abscesses, cysts, or tumors. Early detection makes a significant difference in outcomes.\\n[Read about common health conditions in guinea pigs \u2192](/docs/guinea-pigs/illnesses-and-conditions/common-health-issues)\\n:::"},{"id":"sonnet-one-year-ovarian-cysts","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/sonnet-one-year-ovarian-cysts","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-27-sonnet-one-year/2026-03-27-sonnet-one-year.md","title":"One Year with Sonnet: A Rare Reproductive Condition and a Remarkable Recovery","description":"Today marks one year since Sonnet came into our lives \u2014 and what a year it has been. When she arrived, she was hairless and miserable, her body struggling with a hormonal condition that is genuinely rare even among guinea pig rescuers.","date":"2026-03-27T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"},{"inline":false,"label":"Educational","permalink":"/blog/tags/educational","description":"Educational content"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"}],"readingTime":1.65,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"sonnet-one-year-ovarian-cysts","title":"One Year with Sonnet: A Rare Reproductive Condition and a Remarkable Recovery","date":"2026-03-27T00:00:00.000Z","authors":["danni"],"tags":["guineapigs","medical","educational","sanctuary"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Good News for Story: Just an Infection, Not Something Worse","permalink":"/blog/story-good-news"},"nextItem":{"title":"Meet Villain: A New Friend for a Grieving Guinea Pig Named Hero","permalink":"/blog/villain-meets-hero"}},"content":"Today marks one year since Sonnet came into our lives \u2014 and what a year it has been. When she arrived, she was hairless and miserable, her body struggling with a hormonal condition that is genuinely rare even among guinea pig rescuers.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![Sonnet one year later, healthy and fluffy](./photo-01.jpg)\\n*Sonnet today \u2014 fully recovered, beautifully fluffy, and living her best life with Colin.*\\n\\nSonnet was diagnosed with **ovarian cysts and hyperplasia of the cervical tissue**. This condition accounts for less than 2% of biopsied uteruses in guinea pigs \u2014 it is that uncommon. What made her case even more striking was that Lyric, another guinea pig, came into the rescue with the exact same condition back to back. Two pigs, same rare diagnosis, within weeks of each other.\\n\\nBoth were spayed, and both recovered beautifully.\\n\\nSonnet today is unrecognizable from the hairless, uncomfortable pig who arrived a year ago. She has filled out, grown a gorgeous coat, and settled into life with Colin \u2014 a grumpy old man of a guinea pig who, it turns out, is not so grumpy anymore. At 6.5 years old, Colin is doing tiny popcorns again. That is entirely Sonnet\'s doing.\\n\\nShe is a petite girl and her exact age is difficult to determine because of it, but her spirit is enormous.\\n\\n:::info Ovarian Cysts in Guinea Pigs\\nOvarian cysts are actually one of the most common reproductive conditions in female guinea pigs over two years old, though the specific type Sonnet had \u2014 combined with cervical tissue hyperplasia \u2014 is quite rare. Signs include hair loss along the flanks, a swollen abdomen, and behavioral changes. Spaying is the most effective treatment.\\n\\nIf your guinea pig is showing these signs, please see an exotic vet experienced with small animals.\\n[Read more about Ovarian Cysts in guinea pigs \u2192](/docs/guinea-pigs/illnesses-and-conditions/ovariancysts)\\n:::\\n\\nHappy anniversary, Sonnet. You have no idea how much joy you have brought to this sanctuary \u2014 and to one very grumpy old man. \u{1F338}"},{"id":"villain-meets-hero","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/villain-meets-hero","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-27-villain-meets-hero/2026-03-27-villain-meets-hero.md","title":"Meet Villain: A New Friend for a Grieving Guinea Pig Named Hero","description":"Hero has had a rough year. He is a senior guinea pig who has lost five companions in less than twelve months \u2014 every single one of them a super senior who passed before him. After Quest and Watson crossed the rainbow bridge, Hero became visibly depressed. He stopped being himself.","date":"2026-03-27T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"News","permalink":"/blog/tags/news","description":"News and announcements"}],"readingTime":2.22,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"villain-meets-hero","title":"Meet Villain: A New Friend for a Grieving Guinea Pig Named Hero","date":"2026-03-27T00:00:00.000Z","authors":["danni"],"tags":["guineapigs","sanctuary","news"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"One Year with Sonnet: A Rare Reproductive Condition and a Remarkable Recovery","permalink":"/blog/sonnet-one-year-ovarian-cysts"},"nextItem":{"title":"Casper: The Lethal White Who Started It All","permalink":"/blog/casper-the-lethal-white"}},"content":"Hero has had a rough year. He is a senior guinea pig who has lost five companions in less than twelve months \u2014 every single one of them a super senior who passed before him. After Quest and Watson crossed the rainbow bridge, Hero became visibly depressed. He stopped being himself.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', gap: \'1rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', margin: \'1.5rem 0\'}}>\\n  <img src=\\"./photo-01.jpg\\" alt=\\"Hero and Villain meeting for the first time\\" style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'260px\', borderRadius: \'12px\', objectFit: \'cover\', maxHeight: \'380px\'}} />\\n  <img src=\\"./photo-02.jpg\\" alt=\\"Villain, formerly Pickles\\" style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'260px\', borderRadius: \'12px\', objectFit: \'cover\', maxHeight: \'380px\'}} />\\n</div>\\n*Hero and his new companion Villain \u2014 formerly known as Pickles \u2014 getting acquainted.*\\n\\nAfter visiting Southern Maine Veterinary for Matilda and Story\'s appointments, we made a detour to Auntie Betsy\'s house at [Wheektopia Rescue](https://www.facebook.com/wheektopiarescue). The goal: find Hero a friend who was younger than a super senior, but not a hormonal teenager either. Someone with some staying power who could keep him active and engaged.\\n\\nEnter **Villain** (formerly Pickles).\\n\\nDanni had actually met him not long ago when she and Betsy had back-to-back appointments at Southern Maine, and they both fell in love with each other immediately. At the time, bringing another pig home wasn\'t responsible. But as luck would have it, he was still available \u2014 and he and Hero get along beautifully.\\n\\n![Villain closeup](./photo-03.jpg)\\n*Villain is just the sweetest boy, full of kisses \u2014 despite the name.*\\n\\nHero is already more vibrant. There is a boar glue situation to sort out (anyone who has bonded male guinea pigs knows exactly what this means), but the important thing is that Hero is himself again.\\n\\nDespite the name, Villain is just the sweetest boy, full of kisses. Hero and Villain is, admittedly, a very fun combination.\\n\\n**A note on Twilight:** Betsy also kindly agreed to take on Twilight, who had come into HALT with Moonlight (a satin guinea pig who sadly passed not long after arrival). Twilight was quite aggressive with the special needs pigs in the sanctuary, so the fit just wasn\'t right for him here. Betsy has a number of healthy males, and we are hopeful that Twilight will bond with one of them and find a forever home.\\n\\n:::tip Guinea Pig Bonding\\nGuinea pigs are highly social animals and should not live alone. When introducing new companions, especially adult males, a neutral territory introduction is essential. If you\'re bonding guinea pigs for the first time, our care guide has tips on safe introductions.\\n[Read our Guinea Pig Care Guide \u2192](/docs/guinea-pigs/getting-started/guinea-pig-care-guide)\\n:::"},{"id":"casper-the-lethal-white","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/casper-the-lethal-white","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-26-casper-lethal-white/2026-03-26-casper-lethal-white.md","title":"Casper: The Lethal White Who Started It All","description":"Going through old photos sometimes sends you down a beautiful rabbit hole. This week, Danni found a gem: a photo of Casper, one of the very first intakes when HALT was just getting started \u2014 and the guinea pig who taught her more than she ever expected.","date":"2026-03-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Educational","permalink":"/blog/tags/educational","description":"Educational content"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Lethal White","permalink":"/blog/tags/lethalwhite","description":"Lethal White guinea pigs"}],"readingTime":1.97,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casper-the-lethal-white","title":"Casper: The Lethal White Who Started It All","date":"2026-03-26T00:00:00.000Z","authors":["danni"],"tags":["guineapigs","educational","sanctuary","lethalwhite"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Meet Villain: A New Friend for a Grieving Guinea Pig Named Hero","permalink":"/blog/villain-meets-hero"},"nextItem":{"title":"Goodbye, Clara Bee","permalink":"/blog/goodbye-clara-bee"}},"content":"Going through old photos sometimes sends you down a beautiful rabbit hole. This week, Danni found a gem: a photo of Casper, one of the very first intakes when HALT was just getting started \u2014 and the guinea pig who taught her more than she ever expected.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![Casper the lethal white guinea pig](./photo-01.jpg)\\n*Casper \u2014 one of the very first intakes at HALT, and a little white pig who changed everything.*\\n\\nCasper turned out to be a **lethal white** \u2014 a term that refers to guinea pigs born from two roan or Dalmatian-patterned parents. The genetic combination that produces this coat pattern also causes severe developmental abnormalities: lethal whites are typically blind, deaf, and have malformed digestive systems that make eating and digestion painful and difficult. They require intensive care and rarely live long lives, but with the right support, they can have quality time.\\n\\nAt the time, Danni was brand new to rescue and had no idea what she was looking at. She called her friend and mentor and described the little pig: *\\"This guinea pig is a little off. Is he white? Yes. Does he have red eyes? Yes.\\"* The response was immediate: *\\"What you have is a lethal.\\"*\\n\\nHer mentor laughed \u2014 she had been doing guinea pig rescue for about ten years at that point and had never had a lethal come in. Danni\'s first few weeks, she got one. It must have been a sign of things to come.\\n\\nHer mentor connected her with a friend who specialized in senior, hospice, and special needs small animals, and Casper was officially adopted into expert hands. That connection became a friendship, then a rescue partnership, and Danni learned an enormous amount about caring for animals with complex needs \u2014 knowledge that has shaped the way HALT operates to this day.\\n\\nThat mentor has since retired from rescue and is living her absolute best life. But the gratitude for everything she taught remains.\\n\\n:::info What is a Lethal White Guinea Pig?\\nLethal whites result from breeding two roan or Dalmatian-patterned guinea pigs together. The double-roan gene combination causes serious developmental issues. **Responsible breeders never pair two roan or Dalmatian pigs together for this reason.** If you\'re adopting from a breeder, always ask about their pairing practices.\\n[Read more about Lethal White Guinea Pigs \u2192](/docs/guinea-pigs/breeds-and-genetics/lethalwhite)\\n:::"},{"id":"goodbye-clara-bee","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/goodbye-clara-bee","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-25-clara-bee/2026-03-25-clara-bee.md","title":"Goodbye, Clara Bee","description":"It is with a heavy heart that we share that Clara Bee passed away yesterday after a five-month fight against Congestive Heart Failure \u2014 on top of several other health challenges she had been managing since arriving at the sanctuary in June 2024.","date":"2026-03-25T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Rainbow Bridge","permalink":"/blog/tags/rainbowbridge","description":"Remembering the pets who have crossed the rainbow bridge."},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"}],"readingTime":1.49,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"goodbye-clara-bee","title":"Goodbye, Clara Bee","date":"2026-03-25T00:00:00.000Z","authors":["danni"],"tags":["rainbowbridge","guineapigs","sanctuary","medical"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Casper: The Lethal White Who Started It All","permalink":"/blog/casper-the-lethal-white"},"nextItem":{"title":"Sissy\'s First Supervised Visit with the Big Herd","permalink":"/blog/sissy-meets-the-herd"}},"content":"It is with a heavy heart that we share that Clara Bee passed away yesterday after a five-month fight against Congestive Heart Failure \u2014 on top of several other health challenges she had been managing since arriving at the sanctuary in June 2024.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![Clara Bee](./photo-01.jpg)\\n*Clara Bee \u2014 full of spunk and abbytude right up until the end.*\\n\\nClara Bee was a fighter in every sense of the word. She was full of spunk and abbytude \u2014 that particular brand of Abyssinian guinea pig attitude that is equal parts exasperating and endearing \u2014 right up until her final days. She wasn\'t particularly fond of humans for most of her time with us, which meant we don\'t have as many photos of her as we\'d like. But in her final weeks, something shifted. She decided she loved her medications and would take them willingly, without bolting for the hills. That small act of trust meant everything.\\n\\nAn inner ear infection had left her permanently wobbly, which understandably made being picked up frightening for her. We respected that. We gave her space, we gave her time, and we gave her the best medical care we could.\\n\\nShe leaves behind her sister, **Briella Mae**, who is also managing Congestive Heart Failure. We promise to take the very best care of Briella until the day they are reunited.\\n\\n:::info About Congestive Heart Failure in Guinea Pigs\\nHeart disease is more common in guinea pigs than many people realize, and it can be managed with medication for months or even years. If your guinea pig is showing signs of labored breathing, lethargy, or weight loss, please see an exotic vet promptly.\\n[Read our guide on Heart Disease in Guinea Pigs \u2192](/docs/guinea-pigs/illnesses-and-conditions/heart-disease)\\n:::\\n\\nPopcorn free, sweet girl. \u{1F308}"},{"id":"sissy-meets-the-herd","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/sissy-meets-the-herd","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-25-sissy-herd-intro/2026-03-25-sissy-herd-intro.md","title":"Sissy\'s First Supervised Visit with the Big Herd","description":"Baby Sissy had her first supervised visit with the big herd yesterday, and it went about as well as we could have hoped \u2014 with a few adorable complications.","date":"2026-03-25T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Babies","permalink":"/blog/tags/babies","description":"Baby animals and litters"}],"readingTime":1.64,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"sissy-meets-the-herd","title":"Sissy\'s First Supervised Visit with the Big Herd","date":"2026-03-25T00:00:00.000Z","authors":["danni"],"tags":["guineapigs","sanctuary","babies"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Goodbye, Clara Bee","permalink":"/blog/goodbye-clara-bee"},"nextItem":{"title":"Tonks Finds Her Forever Home \u2014 With the Best Bunny Parents Ever","permalink":"/blog/tonks-finds-her-forever-home"}},"content":"Baby Sissy had her first supervised visit with the big herd yesterday, and it went about as well as we could have hoped \u2014 with a few adorable complications.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![Sissy during her supervised herd visit](./photo-01.jpg)\\n*Sissy during her first supervised visit with the big herd.*\\n\\nSissy is still too small to move in full time. The main concern is the cats \u2014 not that they have ever shown any interest in the guinea pigs, but better safe than sorry when you have a tiny baby in an active herd environment. She will join the big group permanently once she has grown a bit more.\\n\\nIn the meantime, Sissy has been living with Matilda, who is on a modified rest schedule while we sort out her back issues. The two of them have formed an unexpectedly sweet bond. Sissy absolutely adores Matilda, and Matilda \u2014 who can be particular about her companions \u2014 is incredibly patient with her.\\n\\nThe family dynamics during the visit were interesting. Sissy\'s mom, Duchess (named by Alanna, since we already had multiple Fluffys on file), was absolutely over being a mom when it was just the two of them. She was quite rude to Sissy in their small shared space. But in the wide open herd environment, Duchess seemed to genuinely enjoy interacting with her daughter. Space, it turns out, makes a difference.\\n\\nRed Sky, formerly known as Hot Cakes, was absolutely infatuated with Sissy from the moment she arrived. Overall: a very successful outing, and a good sign for Sissy\'s eventual full integration into the herd.\\n\\n:::tip Introducing Young Guinea Pigs to a Herd\\nYoung guinea pigs should be introduced to established herds gradually and with supervision. They need to be large enough that adult pigs cannot accidentally injure them during normal herd activity. Neutral territory introductions and short supervised visits are the safest approach before full integration.\\n[Read our Guinea Pig Care Guide \u2192](/docs/guinea-pigs/getting-started/guinea-pig-care-guide)\\n:::"},{"id":"tonks-finds-her-forever-home","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/tonks-finds-her-forever-home","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-25-tonks-adoption/2026-03-25-tonks-adoption.md","title":"Tonks Finds Her Forever Home \u2014 With the Best Bunny Parents Ever","description":"Sometimes the stars align in the most perfect way. Our friends at NYACC reached out looking for placement for a Californian bunny who had been waiting at one of their shelters for a long time. And as luck would have it, Danni\'s mom had been wanting a Californian bunny for ages.","date":"2026-03-25T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Adoptions","permalink":"/blog/tags/adoptions","description":"Adoption updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Success Stories","permalink":"/blog/tags/success-stories","description":"Adoption success stories and happy endings"},{"inline":false,"label":"Rabbits","permalink":"/blog/tags/rabbits","description":"Rabbit updates and stories"}],"readingTime":1.29,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"tonks-finds-her-forever-home","title":"Tonks Finds Her Forever Home \u2014 With the Best Bunny Parents Ever","date":"2026-03-25T00:00:00.000Z","authors":["danni"],"tags":["adoptions","success-stories","rabbits"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Sissy\'s First Supervised Visit with the Big Herd","permalink":"/blog/sissy-meets-the-herd"},"nextItem":{"title":"Happy National Chinchilla Day from Our Sanctuary Chins!","permalink":"/blog/national-chinchilla-day"}},"content":"Sometimes the stars align in the most perfect way. Our friends at NYACC reached out looking for placement for a Californian bunny who had been waiting at one of their shelters for a long time. And as luck would have it, Danni\'s mom had been wanting a Californian bunny for ages.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', gap: \'1rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', margin: \'1.5rem 0\'}}>\\n  <img src=\\"./photo-01.jpg\\" alt=\\"Tonks at the shelter\\" style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'280px\', borderRadius: \'12px\', objectFit: \'cover\', maxHeight: \'400px\'}} />\\n  <img src=\\"./photo-02.jpg\\" alt=\\"Tonks closeup\\" style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'280px\', borderRadius: \'12px\', objectFit: \'cover\', maxHeight: \'400px\'}} />\\n</div>\\n*Tonks in her shelter photos \u2014 she had been waiting a long time for the right family.*\\n\\nThat was a no-brainer. Tonks \u2014 her shelter name, which may or may not stick \u2014 made the easiest transfer we\'ve had in a while, going straight from NYACC to the absolute best bunny parents in the business. Danni\'s parents have a long and wonderful track record with rabbits, and Tonks is going to be very, very loved.\\n\\nShe\'s still settling in, so we\'ll post more photos once she\'s comfortable and showing off her full personality. But for now: **welcome to the good life, Tonks!** \u{1F407}\\n\\n:::tip Thinking about adopting a rabbit?\\nRabbits are wonderful companions but often misunderstood. They have complex social, dietary, and environmental needs \u2014 and they can live 8\u201312 years! Before adopting, check out our [Rabbit Care Guide](/docs/Rabbits/getting-started/basic-care) to make sure a bunny is the right fit for your home.\\n:::"},{"id":"national-chinchilla-day","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/national-chinchilla-day","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-23-national-chinchilla-day/2026-03-23-national-chinchilla-day.md","title":"Happy National Chinchilla Day from Our Sanctuary Chins!","description":"Did you know that March 23rd is National Chinchilla Day? We couldn\'t let it pass without introducing you to some of the very special chinchillas living in our New Hampshire sanctuary!","date":"2026-03-23T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Chinchillas","permalink":"/blog/tags/chinchillas","description":"Chinchilla updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Educational","permalink":"/blog/tags/educational","description":"Educational content"}],"readingTime":1.87,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"national-chinchilla-day","title":"Happy National Chinchilla Day from Our Sanctuary Chins!","date":"2026-03-23T00:00:00.000Z","authors":["danni"],"tags":["chinchillas","sanctuary","educational"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Tonks Finds Her Forever Home \u2014 With the Best Bunny Parents Ever","permalink":"/blog/tonks-finds-her-forever-home"},"nextItem":{"title":"Vet Update: Halloween\'s Abscess and Sissy\'s Juvenile Cataracts","permalink":"/blog/halloween-sissy-march-update"}},"content":"Did you know that March 23rd is National Chinchilla Day? We couldn\'t let it pass without introducing you to some of the very special chinchillas living in our New Hampshire sanctuary!\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nMost of our sanctuary chinchillas have Grey\'s Anatomy-inspired names \u2014 with a few notable exceptions. Morticia, Gomez, and Wednesday have their own story (that\'s a whole other post!). Each of these animals came to us with histories that made rehoming difficult, whether due to age, health challenges, or simply needing a quieter, more permanent arrangement than foster life could provide.\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', gap: \'1rem\', margin: \'1.5rem 0\'}}>\\n  <img src=\\"./photo-01.jpg\\" alt=\\"Sanctuary chinchilla\\" style={{width: \'calc(50% - 0.5rem)\', borderRadius: \'12px\', objectFit: \'cover\', aspectRatio: \'1\'}} />\\n  <img src=\\"./photo-02.jpg\\" alt=\\"Sanctuary chinchilla\\" style={{width: \'calc(50% - 0.5rem)\', borderRadius: \'12px\', objectFit: \'cover\', aspectRatio: \'1\'}} />\\n  <img src=\\"./photo-03.jpg\\" alt=\\"Sanctuary chinchilla\\" style={{width: \'calc(50% - 0.5rem)\', borderRadius: \'12px\', objectFit: \'cover\', aspectRatio: \'1\'}} />\\n  <img src=\\"./photo-04.jpg\\" alt=\\"Sanctuary chinchilla\\" style={{width: \'calc(50% - 0.5rem)\', borderRadius: \'12px\', objectFit: \'cover\', aspectRatio: \'1\'}} />\\n  <img src=\\"./photo-05.jpg\\" alt=\\"Sanctuary chinchilla\\" style={{width: \'calc(50% - 0.5rem)\', borderRadius: \'12px\', objectFit: \'cover\', aspectRatio: \'1\'}} />\\n  <img src=\\"./photo-06.jpg\\" alt=\\"Sanctuary chinchilla\\" style={{width: \'calc(50% - 0.5rem)\', borderRadius: \'12px\', objectFit: \'cover\', aspectRatio: \'1\'}} />\\n</div>\\n\\nChinchillas are often misunderstood as low-maintenance pets, but they are actually quite complex animals with specific environmental, dietary, and social needs. They can live 15 years or more, are highly sensitive to heat, and require dust baths, not water baths. They are most active at dawn and dusk, which makes them a wonderful match for night-owl households \u2014 but less ideal for young children who want a cuddly daytime companion.\\n\\n:::tip Want to learn more about chinchillas?\\nWe have a comprehensive care guide on our resources page covering everything from diet and housing to common health conditions. [Read our Chinchilla Care Guide \u2192](/docs/Chinchillas/getting-started/chinchilla-care-guide)\\n:::\\n\\nIf you have chinchillas of your own, give them a gentle smooch and an extra (but healthy!) treat today. And if you\'ve ever been curious about whether a chinchilla might be the right fit for your home, our resources are a great place to start.\\n\\n\u{1F42D}\u{1F4A8}"},{"id":"halloween-sissy-march-update","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/halloween-sissy-march-update","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-13-halloween-sissy-update/2026-03-13-halloween-sissy-update.md","title":"Vet Update: Halloween\'s Abscess and Sissy\'s Juvenile Cataracts","description":"Yesterday was a big vet day, and we have updates on two of our special needs girls: Halloween and baby Sissy.","date":"2026-03-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"}],"readingTime":1.84,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"halloween-sissy-march-update","title":"Vet Update: Halloween\'s Abscess and Sissy\'s Juvenile Cataracts","date":"2026-03-13T00:00:00.000Z","authors":["danni"],"tags":["guineapigs","medical","sanctuary"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Happy National Chinchilla Day from Our Sanctuary Chins!","permalink":"/blog/national-chinchilla-day"},"nextItem":{"title":"Baby Rat Report: The Long Island Nursery Update","permalink":"/blog/baby-rat-report-long-island-nursery"}},"content":"Yesterday was a big vet day, and we have updates on two of our special needs girls: Halloween and baby Sissy.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', gap: \'1rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', margin: \'1.5rem 0\'}}>\\n  <img src=\\"./photo-01.jpg\\" alt=\\"Halloween recovering from her abscess\\" style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'260px\', borderRadius: \'12px\', objectFit: \'cover\', maxHeight: \'380px\'}} />\\n  <img src=\\"./photo-02.jpg\\" alt=\\"Baby Sissy\\" style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'260px\', borderRadius: \'12px\', objectFit: \'cover\', maxHeight: \'380px\'}} />\\n</div>\\n\\n## Halloween: Abscess and Healing\\n\\nHalloween has been through a lot. After her mammary tumor surgery, her incision site abscessed and burst, leaving a sizeable wound on her belly. It looked alarming \u2014 and honestly, it was. But the good news is that it is healing remarkably well. Dr. Ford is very pleased with her progress.\\n\\nThe concern going forward is that Halloween seems to abscess and develop infections more easily than average, which raises questions about her overall immune system. We are monitoring her closely and hoping that she will not need further surgeries. She is a resilient girl, and she has already proven she can bounce back from a lot.\\n\\n## Sissy: Juvenile Cataracts\\n\\nBaby Sissy\'s eyes have developed a cloudiness that we noticed recently \u2014 the kind you typically see in much older guinea pigs. After her exam, the diagnosis is **juvenile cataracts**. Whatever vision she currently has is likely blurry, and there is a possibility that she may go completely blind as she grows.\\n\\nThe good news is that Sissy is otherwise in excellent health. Guinea pigs adapt remarkably well to vision loss, relying on their other senses \u2014 particularly their sense of smell and spatial memory \u2014 to navigate their environment. With a stable, consistent living space and attentive care, blind guinea pigs can live full and happy lives.\\n\\nWe will continue to monitor her vision and make any adjustments to her environment as needed.\\n\\n:::info Vision Loss in Guinea Pigs\\nGuinea pigs can develop cataracts from genetics, aging, or certain infections. Blind guinea pigs generally adapt well when their environment is kept consistent \u2014 avoid rearranging their space frequently, and use scent cues to help them orient. If you notice cloudiness in your guinea pig\'s eyes, have them evaluated by an exotic vet.\\n:::"},{"id":"baby-rat-report-long-island-nursery","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/baby-rat-report-long-island-nursery","source":"@site/blog/2026-02-25-baby-rat-report/2026-02-25-baby-rat-report.md","title":"Baby Rat Report: The Long Island Nursery Update","description":"Alanna here, giving you today\'s baby rat update from the Long Island nursery. Things are progressing beautifully \u2014 and adorably.","date":"2026-02-25T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Rats","permalink":"/blog/tags/rats","description":"Rat updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Intake","permalink":"/blog/tags/intake","description":"New animals coming into the rescue"},{"inline":false,"label":"Babies","permalink":"/blog/tags/babies","description":"Baby animals and litters"}],"readingTime":1.81,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Alanna Burke","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/alanna"},"imageURL":"/img/alanna.jpg","key":"alanna"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"baby-rat-report-long-island-nursery","title":"Baby Rat Report: The Long Island Nursery Update","authors":["alanna"],"tags":["rats","intake","babies"],"image":"./photo5.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Vet Update: Halloween\'s Abscess and Sissy\'s Juvenile Cataracts","permalink":"/blog/halloween-sissy-march-update"},"nextItem":{"title":"The Long Island Rat Rescue: Dozens of Lives Saved","permalink":"/blog/long-island-rat-hoarding-rescue"}},"content":"Alanna here, giving you today\'s baby rat update from the Long Island nursery. Things are progressing beautifully \u2014 and adorably.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Water Bottles and Sibling Rivalry\\n\\nThe most exciting development this week: the precocious ones have learned to use the water bottle. They have also learned to fight over the water bottle, which, honestly, is a more essential rat life skill. They will spend far more of their adult lives fighting over water bottles than actually drinking from them. I consider this a developmental milestone.\\n\\n<div className=\\"row\\" style={{display: \'flex\', gap: \'0.75rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1.5rem\'}}>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'180px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo1.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Baby rats\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'180px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo2.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Baby rats at water bottle\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'180px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo3.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Baby rats growing\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\n## The Adult Rats Are Testing Me\\n\\nThe adult rats have also been testing my patience this week. They have developed a strong preference for the two smaller, more annoying-to-fill water bottles over the three large, easy-to-fill bottles I have provided for their convenience.\\n\\nIt is as if they know. They want to make more work for me. I respect the commitment to chaos.\\n\\n<div className=\\"row\\" style={{display: \'flex\', gap: \'1rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1.5rem\'}}>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'250px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo4.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Adult rats\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'250px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo5.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Rat group\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\n## The Cuteness Carries Us\\n\\nTheir overwhelming cuteness is genuinely what is getting me through the logistical challenges of caring for this many rats at once. I just want to snuggle them all day. I hope we can raise them all to be big, healthy, happy rats who go on to wonderful homes.\\n\\nSend the babies your love \u2014 and the mommas your support. They are working very hard to care for all these little ones. \u{1F400}\u{1F49B}\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"long-island-rat-hoarding-rescue","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/long-island-rat-hoarding-rescue","source":"@site/blog/2026-02-23-long-island-rats/2026-02-23-long-island-rats.md","title":"The Long Island Rat Rescue: Dozens of Lives Saved","description":"I am losing my mind over how cute these babies are, and I need you all to know about them.","date":"2026-02-23T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Rescue Operations","permalink":"/blog/tags/rescue","description":"Large-scale rescues and intake operations"},{"inline":false,"label":"Rats","permalink":"/blog/tags/rats","description":"Rat updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Intake","permalink":"/blog/tags/intake","description":"New animals coming into the rescue"}],"readingTime":3.73,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Alanna Burke","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/alanna"},"imageURL":"/img/alanna.jpg","key":"alanna"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"long-island-rat-hoarding-rescue","title":"The Long Island Rat Rescue: Dozens of Lives Saved","authors":["alanna"],"tags":["rescue","rats","intake"],"image":"./photo2.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Baby Rat Report: The Long Island Nursery Update","permalink":"/blog/baby-rat-report-long-island-nursery"},"nextItem":{"title":"Tally and the Adequan Experiment: Managing Arthritis in Senior Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tally-adequan-arthritis-guinea-pigs"}},"content":"I am losing my mind over how cute these babies are, and I need you all to know about them.\\n\\nWe pulled a few dozen rats from [Strong Island Animal Rescue League](https://www.facebook.com/strongislandanimalrescueleague), who have been working on a monumental pet rat hoarding case on Long Island. When they reached out asking if we could help, I figured: who needs a kitchen? I set up an enormous cage and took in as many as I possibly could.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The Situation\\n\\nThis was a large-scale hoarding case involving a significant number of pet rats living in conditions that were not appropriate for their care. Strong Island Animal Rescue League has been doing incredible work managing the removal and initial care of these animals, and we are honored to be one of the rescues they trusted to take some of them in.\\n\\n<div className=\\"row\\" style={{display: \'flex\', gap: \'0.75rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1.5rem\'}}>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'180px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo1.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Rats arriving\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'180px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo2.jpg\').default} alt=\\"The rat crew\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'180px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo3.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Rat babies\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\n## Teen Moms and Tiny Babies\\n\\nThese ladies have had a terribly rough start in life, and it is going to take them a while to decompress. They are slowly relaxing and no longer hiding every time I walk into the room \u2014 which is real progress.\\n\\nSome of them came in pregnant, and teen moms are teen moms. They are not always the most attentive mothers, but the babies depend on them. I had to track down the mama rats and move them into a smaller nursery cage, because in the big group cage they were squirreling away babies or running while nursing and a baby would drop off and get lost.\\n\\nI know the mamas hate the close quarters, but until the babies are bigger, it has to be this way.\\n\\n<div className=\\"row\\" style={{display: \'flex\', gap: \'0.75rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1.5rem\'}}>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'180px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo4.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Baby rats\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'180px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo5.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Baby rats nursing\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'180px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo6.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Baby rats in nest\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\n## The Technicolor Babies\\n\\nDo not be alarmed by the colorful babies \u2014 that is food dye that I applied to track their individual weights. When you have a litter of babies who all look similar, color coding is the only practical way to make sure each one is being weighed accurately and gaining appropriately.\\n\\nThe biggest babies are already using the water bottle, and I am so proud of them.\\n\\n<div className=\\"row\\" style={{display: \'flex\', gap: \'0.75rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1.5rem\'}}>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'180px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo7.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Colorful baby rats\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'180px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo8.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Baby rat closeup\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'180px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo9.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Baby rats growing\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\n## What These Rats Need\\n\\nThese ladies have had a terribly rough start in life, and it is going to take time and patience to help them decompress and learn to trust people. But they are already making progress, and we believe that with the right care, they will become wonderful companions.\\n\\n![The full crew settling in](photo10.jpg)\\n*The crew, slowly learning that humans can be trusted.*\\n\\n:::tip Thinking About Adopting a Rat?\\nRats are incredibly intelligent, social, and affectionate animals. Learn everything you need to know in our [rat care guide](/docs/Rats/getting-started/rat-care-guide) and find out [if a rat is right for you](/docs/Rats/getting-started/is-a-rat-right-for-me).\\n:::\\n\\nWe will need a lot of rat fosters as the boys grow up and need to be separated, and a lot of rat adopters when they are ready for homes. If you have been considering adding rats to your family, now is a great time to reach out! \u{1F400}\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"tally-adequan-arthritis-guinea-pigs","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/tally-adequan-arthritis-guinea-pigs","source":"@site/blog/2026-02-17-tally-arthritis/2026-02-17-tally-arthritis.md","title":"Tally and the Adequan Experiment: Managing Arthritis in Senior Guinea Pigs","description":"This is Tally \u2014 our second oldest guinea pig in the rescue, and one of the animals who inspired us to try something new in our approach to arthritis management. We want to share what we are learning, because it may be useful to other guinea pig owners managing senior animals.","date":"2026-02-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"},{"inline":false,"label":"Educational","permalink":"/blog/tags/educational","description":"Educational content"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"}],"readingTime":2.81,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"tally-adequan-arthritis-guinea-pigs","title":"Tally and the Adequan Experiment: Managing Arthritis in Senior Guinea Pigs","authors":["danni"],"tags":["medical","educational","guineapigs","sanctuary"],"image":"./photo1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"The Long Island Rat Rescue: Dozens of Lives Saved","permalink":"/blog/long-island-rat-hoarding-rescue"},"nextItem":{"title":"Ophelia: Eight Years of Joy","permalink":"/blog/ophelia-eight-years-of-joy"}},"content":"This is Tally \u2014 our second oldest guinea pig in the rescue, and one of the animals who inspired us to try something new in our approach to arthritis management. We want to share what we are learning, because it may be useful to other guinea pig owners managing senior animals.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![Tally](photo1.jpg)\\n*Tally, our wise and well-loved senior.*\\n\\n## Tally\'s Background\\n\\nTally is a sweet old lady who has been with us for a long time. She needed to put on some weight when she arrived, but in this house, that was easy to fix. Over time, she developed bony changes \u2014 particularly in her ankles. The changes were significant enough that I could feel the difference when handling her, which is not something I can normally detect.\\n\\nShe has been on oral arthritis medication for a while now, and it has helped. But oral medications for guinea pigs come with some drawbacks that we have been thinking about carefully.\\n\\n## The Problem with Long-Term Oral Medications\\n\\nDaily oral medications for guinea pigs are effective, but they have real limitations:\\n\\n- They can be hard on the **kidneys and liver** over the long term\\n- They require **daily administration** \u2014 which adds up to hours of time each week across multiple animals\\n- Guinea pigs often resist them, making the process stressful for both animal and caregiver\\n- We go through them **very quickly** when managing multiple patients\\n\\nWhen you have a rescue with multiple senior animals on arthritis management, these limitations become significant.\\n\\n## Enter Adequan\\n\\nWe had a very interesting conversation with our veterinarian about **Adequan** \u2014 an injectable arthritis medication typically used for dogs, but showing a lot of promise (and zero side effects so far) for guinea pigs.\\n\\n![Tally resting comfortably](photo2.jpg)\\n*Tally in one of her favorite resting spots.*\\n\\nHere is why we are excited about it:\\n\\n**The schedule:** After an initial loading period, guinea pigs would only need injections every three weeks. Compare that to oral medication every single day, and the time savings are enormous.\\n\\n**The dosage:** Guinea pigs need only a tiny amount \u2014 far less than dogs. This means a supply lasts much longer than oral medications.\\n\\n**The side effect profile:** So far, the research and clinical experience suggest Adequan is very well tolerated in guinea pigs with no significant side effects.\\n\\n**The potential for satin guinea pigs:** We are particularly curious about how Adequan will work for our satin guinea pigs with osteodystrophy \u2014 a bone density condition associated with the satin gene. This is an area where we are hoping to gather meaningful data.\\n\\n## Our Plan\\n\\nWe are starting with a small sample and documenting each guinea pig\'s progress carefully. (Yes, this means more spreadsheets. I genuinely love spreadsheets.) As we see results, we will add more animals to the trial.\\n\\nThis is exactly the kind of thing we mean when we talk about following the research and keeping an open mind as veterinary science evolves. We are not just managing these animals \u2014 we are learning alongside their vets and contributing to the growing body of knowledge about exotic mammal care.\\n\\n:::tip Satin Syndrome Resources\\nIf you have a satin guinea pig, learn more about the genetic condition and what it means for long-term care in our [Satin Syndrome guide](/docs/guinea-pigs/breeds-and-genetics/satinsyndrome).\\n:::\\n\\nWe will keep sharing updates as we learn more. \u{1F49B}\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"ophelia-eight-years-of-joy","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/ophelia-eight-years-of-joy","source":"@site/blog/2026-02-15-ophelia/2026-02-15-ophelia.md","title":"Ophelia: Eight Years of Joy","description":"Our sweet senior, Ophelia, passed away yesterday. She had been with me for almost four years, putting her at around eight years old \u2014 a remarkable age for a guinea pig. She was one of the most joyful animals I have ever had the privilege of caring for.","date":"2026-02-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Rainbow Bridge","permalink":"/blog/tags/rainbowbridge","description":"Remembering the pets who have crossed the rainbow bridge."},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"}],"readingTime":2.17,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"ophelia-eight-years-of-joy","title":"Ophelia: Eight Years of Joy","authors":["danni"],"tags":["rainbowbridge","guineapigs","sanctuary"],"image":"./photo1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Tally and the Adequan Experiment: Managing Arthritis in Senior Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tally-adequan-arthritis-guinea-pigs"},"nextItem":{"title":"Halloween\'s Mammary Tumor: The Importance of Early Detection","permalink":"/blog/halloween-mammary-tumor-early-detection"}},"content":"Our sweet senior, Ophelia, passed away yesterday. She had been with me for almost four years, putting her at around eight years old \u2014 a remarkable age for a guinea pig. She was one of the most joyful animals I have ever had the privilege of caring for.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![Ophelia](photo1.jpg)\\n*Ophelia, the happiest girl in the world.*\\n\\n## Her Story\\n\\nOphelia had a neurological event years ago that left her very wobbly and blind. For many animals, that kind of disability would mean a diminished quality of life. For Ophelia, it barely slowed her down.\\n\\nShe remained the happiest girl in the world. She navigated her space by memory and by feel. She knew where her hay was, where her pellets were, and where the best spots to nap were. She adapted completely, and she thrived.\\n\\n<div className=\\"row\\" style={{display: \'flex\', gap: \'1rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1.5rem\'}}>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'200px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo2.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Ophelia resting\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'200px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo3.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Ophelia being held\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\n## Our Special Time\\n\\nOver the past year, I set aside dedicated time every day just to sit with Ophelia. I would give her her arthritis medication and then we would sit together in the rocking chair and listen to music. She would settle in, relax, and just be present. Those moments were some of the most peaceful of my day.\\n\\nI am so grateful we had that time together. I am so grateful she got to live eight full years, and that the last four of them were spent being loved and cared for every single day.\\n\\n<div className=\\"row\\" style={{display: \'flex\', gap: \'1rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1.5rem\'}}>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'200px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo4.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Ophelia in her favorite spot\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'200px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo5.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Ophelia closeup\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\n![Ophelia\'s last days](photo6.jpg)\\n*Ophelia in her favorite spot, right where she belonged.*\\n\\n## Why Sanctuary Matters\\n\\nOphelia is exactly why we do sanctuary care. She was a senior animal with a neurological condition and arthritis. Many rescues and shelters would not have had the capacity or the knowledge to manage her needs. We did, and because of that, she got four more years of a good life.\\n\\nThat is what this work is about.\\n\\nPopcorn free, sweet girl. We will miss every little thing about you. \u{1F308}\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"halloween-mammary-tumor-early-detection","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/halloween-mammary-tumor-early-detection","source":"@site/blog/2026-02-13-halloween-tumor/2026-02-13-halloween-tumor.md","title":"Halloween\'s Mammary Tumor: The Importance of Early Detection","description":"We have an update on Halloween, one of our young sanctuary guinea pigs, and while it is not the news we were hoping for, it is much better than it could have been \u2014 and it is a story worth sharing about the importance of catching things early.","date":"2026-02-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"}],"readingTime":2.03,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"halloween-mammary-tumor-early-detection","title":"Halloween\'s Mammary Tumor: The Importance of Early Detection","authors":["danni"],"tags":["medical","guineapigs","sanctuary"],"image":"./photo1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Ophelia: Eight Years of Joy","permalink":"/blog/ophelia-eight-years-of-joy"},"nextItem":{"title":"February is Pet Dental Health Month: A Cautionary Tale","permalink":"/blog/pet-dental-health-month-malocclusion"}},"content":"We have an update on Halloween, one of our young sanctuary guinea pigs, and while it is not the news we were hoping for, it is much better than it could have been \u2014 and it is a story worth sharing about the importance of catching things early.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![Halloween](photo1.jpg)\\n*Halloween, only 5 months old and already showing us what a fighter she is.*\\n\\n## The Discovery\\n\\nHalloween is only 5 months old. During a routine check, we noticed a mass developing. We had it tested right away, and the results came back: it is a **mammary tumor**, but at this time it is showing only inflammation cells and mammary tissue \u2014 not malignant cancer cells.\\n\\nThat is the best possible news given the circumstances.\\n\\nHowever, in just two days, the mass changed dramatically in size, shape, and texture. That kind of rapid change tells us we need to act. We are anticipating needing to remove it surgically.\\n\\n## Why We Test Everything\\n\\nSome people might wonder why we bother testing a mass in a 5-month-old guinea pig. The answer is simple: **we test everything, because knowing what we are dealing with changes how we treat it.**\\n\\nA benign mass that is growing rapidly still needs to come out \u2014 but knowing it is not currently malignant gives us more flexibility in timing and approach. If it were malignant, we would be moving even faster.\\n\\n![Halloween resting](photo2.jpg)\\n*Resting up before her procedure.*\\n\\n## Mammary Tumors in Guinea Pigs\\n\\nMammary tumors are not uncommon in guinea pigs, and they can occur in both females and males. They can be benign or malignant, and the only way to know which is through testing. A mass that appears benign can also change over time, which is why monitoring and early intervention are so important.\\n\\nSigns to watch for in your guinea pig:\\n- Any new lump or bump, especially along the belly or chest\\n- A mass that changes in size, shape, or texture\\n- Skin changes around a lump (redness, ulceration, discharge)\\n- Changes in appetite or behavior\\n\\n:::tip Cancer Resources\\nLearn more about tumors and cancer in guinea pigs in our [guinea pig cancer guide](/docs/guinea-pigs/illnesses-and-conditions/cancer). Early detection and prompt veterinary care make a significant difference in outcomes.\\n:::\\n\\nWe will keep you updated on Halloween\'s recovery. She is a tough little girl, and we are in her corner every step of the way. \u{1F49B}\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"pet-dental-health-month-malocclusion","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/pet-dental-health-month-malocclusion","source":"@site/blog/2026-02-05-dental-awareness/2026-02-05-dental-awareness.md","title":"February is Pet Dental Health Month: A Cautionary Tale","description":"February is Pet Dental Health Month, and our friends at Wheektopia Guinea Pig Care and Rescue Center have given us permission to share an extreme case they are currently managing. PB\'s story is a powerful reminder of why routine wellness exams matter \u2014 even for the smallest of animals.","date":"2026-02-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"},{"inline":false,"label":"Educational","permalink":"/blog/tags/educational","description":"Educational content"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Dental","permalink":"/blog/tags/dental","description":"Dental health topics"}],"readingTime":2.82,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"pet-dental-health-month-malocclusion","title":"February is Pet Dental Health Month: A Cautionary Tale","authors":["danni"],"tags":["medical","educational","guineapigs","dental"],"image":"./photo1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Halloween\'s Mammary Tumor: The Importance of Early Detection","permalink":"/blog/halloween-mammary-tumor-early-detection"},"nextItem":{"title":"Caedence\'s Heart: A Hoarding Case Comeback Story","permalink":"/blog/caedence-heart-hoarding-case-comeback"}},"content":"February is Pet Dental Health Month, and our friends at [Wheektopia Guinea Pig Care and Rescue Center](https://www.facebook.com/Wheektopia) have given us permission to share an extreme case they are currently managing. PB\'s story is a powerful reminder of why routine wellness exams matter \u2014 even for the smallest of animals.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## PB\'s Story\\n\\nPB was surrendered to Wheektopia with **chronic malocclusion** \u2014 a condition where the teeth do not align properly and continue to overgrow in painful, damaging ways. He has to get his teeth trimmed every single month to prevent them from reaching the state shown in the before photo below.\\n\\n<div className=\\"row\\" style={{display: \'flex\', gap: \'1rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1.5rem\', alignItems: \'flex-start\'}}>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'220px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo1.jpg\').default} alt=\\"PB\'s teeth before trimming\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n    <p style={{fontSize: \'0.85rem\', color: \'#666\', marginTop: \'0.5rem\', textAlign: \'center\'}}><em>Before: severe overgrowth and malocclusion</em></p>\\n  </div>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'220px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo2.jpg\').default} alt=\\"PB\'s teeth after trimming\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n    <p style={{fontSize: \'0.85rem\', color: \'#666\', marginTop: \'0.5rem\', textAlign: \'center\'}}><em>After: trimmed and much more comfortable</em></p>\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\nThis is one of the many reasons we advocate so strongly for **routine wellness checks** for even the smallest of critters.\\n\\n## Why Early Detection Matters So Much\\n\\nWhen dental issues are caught early \u2014 when the molars only have small points starting to develop \u2014 the correction is relatively minor. A simple trim, a little discomfort, and the guinea pig is back to eating normally within a day or two.\\n\\nWhen dental issues are left untreated, the overgrown teeth cause jaw pain so severe that the guinea pig stops eating hay. And hay is the primary thing that keeps guinea pig teeth worn down naturally. So the problem compounds: the more it hurts to chew hay, the less hay they eat, and the faster the teeth overgrow. It becomes a cycle that is very difficult to break.\\n\\n![PB recovering](photo3.jpg)\\n*PB after his most recent trim \u2014 already looking more comfortable.*\\n\\nBy getting teeth trimmed when there are only small points, there is much less jaw pain in the long run. Guinea pigs who receive regular dental care eat more hay, which keeps their teeth trimmer between appointments, which means less intervention needed over time.\\n\\n## \\"Routine Exams Aren\'t Necessary\\"\\n\\nWe see this sentiment in guinea pig groups far too often, and we want to address it directly: **routine wellness exams are absolutely necessary.**\\n\\nVeterinary science has come leaps and bounds for exotic animals. There are now vets who specialize in small mammals and have the equipment and training to catch dental issues, heart conditions, respiratory problems, and more \u2014 long before they become emergencies. Waiting until your guinea pig is visibly sick often means waiting until they are critically ill.\\n\\n:::tip Our Dental Resources\\nWe have detailed guides on dental health for guinea pigs. Check out our [guinea pig dental disease guide](/docs/guinea-pigs/health-and-wellness/dental-disease) and [dental health overview](/docs/guinea-pigs/health-and-wellness/dental-health) for everything you need to know about keeping your pig\'s teeth healthy.\\n:::\\n\\nDon\'t wait until it\'s too late. Schedule that wellness exam. \u{1F49B}\\n\\n*Thank you to Wheektopia for allowing us to share PB\'s story. You can follow their work on [Facebook](https://www.facebook.com/Wheektopia).*\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"caedence-heart-hoarding-case-comeback","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/caedence-heart-hoarding-case-comeback","source":"@site/blog/2026-01-30-caedence-heart/2026-01-30-caedence-heart.md","title":"Caedence\'s Heart: A Hoarding Case Comeback Story","description":"We got surprisingly wonderful news at the vet this week, and we just have to share it. Caedence is doing better than ever \u2014 and given everything she has been through, that is nothing short of remarkable.","date":"2026-01-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"}],"readingTime":1.51,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"caedence-heart-hoarding-case-comeback","title":"Caedence\'s Heart: A Hoarding Case Comeback Story","authors":["danni"],"tags":["medical","guineapigs","sanctuary"],"image":"./photo1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"February is Pet Dental Health Month: A Cautionary Tale","permalink":"/blog/pet-dental-health-month-malocclusion"},"nextItem":{"title":"Ducky Joins the Herd: A Happy Ending for Our Box Baby","permalink":"/blog/ducky-joins-the-herd"}},"content":"We got surprisingly wonderful news at the vet this week, and we just have to share it. Caedence is doing better than ever \u2014 and given everything she has been through, that is nothing short of remarkable.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![Caedence](photo1.jpg)\\n*Caedence, thriving.*\\n\\n## Where She Came From\\n\\nCaedence came to us in fall 2024 from a hoarding case. She was pregnant when she arrived, and that pregnancy was the most precarious one we have ever managed. So many sleepless nights hand-feeding her. So many emergency vet trips. It was utterly exhausting \u2014 but we were not going to give up on her.\\n\\nShe has a **heart condition**, and her pregnancy greatly exacerbated it. Managing a pregnant guinea pig with a cardiac condition is genuinely one of the most stressful things we have done. But she made it. Her babies were born, and once they were weaned, her heart condition began to calm down.\\n\\n## The Good News\\n\\nAt her general wellness exam this week, her heart is doing **even better than on her last exam**. Her weight is great. Her overall condition is excellent.\\n\\nThis is the kind of update that makes every sleepless night worth it.\\n\\nWe are also staying in touch with the families who adopted her babies, so we can monitor whether any of them develop heart issues as they age. Heart conditions in guinea pigs can have a genetic component, and we want to make sure their adopters are informed and watching.\\n\\n:::info Guinea Pig Heart Disease\\nHeart disease is more common in guinea pigs than many people realize. Learn about the signs to watch for and how it is managed in our [guinea pig heart disease resource](/docs/guinea-pigs/illnesses-and-conditions/common-health-issues).\\n:::\\n\\nYou are a fighter, Caedence. We are so proud of you. \u{1F49B}\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"ducky-joins-the-herd","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/ducky-joins-the-herd","source":"@site/blog/2026-01-30-ducky/2026-01-30-ducky.md","title":"Ducky Joins the Herd: A Happy Ending for Our Box Baby","description":"For those of you who remember back in October when we had that group of piggies that was handed to Jen in a box \u2014 this is Ducky\'s update, and it is a good one.","date":"2026-01-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Adoptions","permalink":"/blog/tags/adoptions","description":"Adoption updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Success Stories","permalink":"/blog/tags/success-stories","description":"Adoption success stories and happy endings"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":1.58,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jen Dupee","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/jen"},"imageURL":"/img/jen.jpg","key":"jen"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"ducky-joins-the-herd","title":"Ducky Joins the Herd: A Happy Ending for Our Box Baby","authors":["jen"],"tags":["adoptions","success-stories","guineapigs"],"image":"./photo1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Caedence\'s Heart: A Hoarding Case Comeback Story","permalink":"/blog/caedence-heart-hoarding-case-comeback"},"nextItem":{"title":"Meet Mr. Buttons: A 10-Year-Old Rabbit\'s Second Chance","permalink":"/blog/mr-buttons-rabbit-second-chance"}},"content":"For those of you who remember back in October when we had that group of piggies that was handed to Jen in a box \u2014 this is Ducky\'s update, and it is a good one.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![Ducky](photo1.jpg)\\n*Ducky, the biggest personality in the smallest package.*\\n\\n## The Box Babies\\n\\nLast fall, a group of guinea pigs was surrendered to Jen in a box \u2014 an unfortunately common way small animals end up in rescue. Most of that group has since been adopted into wonderful homes. But Ducky, we held back for a while.\\n\\nThere was nothing medically wrong with her. She was just... a lot. The largest of the babies, very sassy, and a bit of a bulldozer. We were concerned that if she went home with one of her siblings, she would just steamroll them. She needed to go somewhere where she could be herself without causing chaos.\\n\\n## The Perfect Solution\\n\\nWe knew exactly what to do: introduce her to our big group of resident females. These ladies would not put up with her nonsense \u2014 but they also would not pick a fight with her. They would simply put her in her place, calmly and firmly, and she would learn.\\n\\nAnd that is exactly what happened.\\n\\n<div className=\\"row\\" style={{display: \'flex\', gap: \'1rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1.5rem\'}}>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'200px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo2.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Ducky with the herd\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'200px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo3.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Ducky settled in\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\nDucky has settled in beautifully with the group. She found her place, she learned the social rules, and she is thriving. Sometimes the best adoption story is the one where an animal finds exactly the right environment to grow into who they are meant to be.\\n\\nWelcome to the herd, Ducky. \u{1F49B}\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"mr-buttons-rabbit-second-chance","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/mr-buttons-rabbit-second-chance","source":"@site/blog/2026-01-17-mr-buttons/2026-01-17-mr-buttons.md","title":"Meet Mr. Buttons: A 10-Year-Old Rabbit\'s Second Chance","description":"Meet the newest member of our sanctuary family: Mr. Buttons. He is approximately 10 years old, he is cute as an absolute button, and he has had a very rough go of it \u2014 but that is all about to change.","date":"2026-01-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Rabbits","permalink":"/blog/tags/rabbits","description":"Rabbit updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Intake","permalink":"/blog/tags/intake","description":"New animals coming into the rescue"}],"readingTime":1.8,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"mr-buttons-rabbit-second-chance","title":"Meet Mr. Buttons: A 10-Year-Old Rabbit\'s Second Chance","authors":["danni"],"tags":["sanctuary","rabbits","intake"],"image":"./photo1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Ducky Joins the Herd: A Happy Ending for Our Box Baby","permalink":"/blog/ducky-joins-the-herd"},"nextItem":{"title":"Julius Cheeser: From Storm Drain to Forever Home","permalink":"/blog/julius-cheeser-forever-home"}},"content":"Meet the newest member of our sanctuary family: Mr. Buttons. He is approximately 10 years old, he is cute as an absolute button, and he has had a very rough go of it \u2014 but that is all about to change.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![Mr. Buttons](photo1.jpg)\\n*Mr. Buttons, settling in and already stealing hearts.*\\n\\n## His Story\\n\\nMr. Buttons came to us through our friends at RBARI. His previous family had him for his entire life \u2014 about 10 years \u2014 but there were two significant problems with his care that have left him in rough shape.\\n\\nFirst, his family thought he was a girl for all 10 years they had him. This is not uncommon with rabbits, as sexing them can be tricky, but it meant he never received any reproductive health care appropriate for a male rabbit.\\n\\nSecond, and more critically, he was fed **guinea pig pellets** for the entirety of his life. Guinea pig pellets are formulated for guinea pigs, not rabbits \u2014 they have very different nutritional profiles. Rabbits fed an improper diet over many years can develop serious health issues related to malnutrition and organ stress.\\n\\nCombined with his age and the stress of surrender, Mr. Buttons was not doing well when he came to us.\\n\\n![Mr. Buttons resting](photo2.jpg)\\n*Already looking more comfortable.*\\n\\n## His Future\\n\\nMr. Buttons will either be a permanent sanctuary member with Jen or Danni. We are not rushing anything \u2014 his first priority is getting the proper nutrition, veterinary care, and rest he needs to start feeling better.\\n\\nSenior rabbits can live very comfortably with the right care, and we are committed to giving Mr. Buttons the best possible quality of life for whatever time he has left. He deserves to know what it feels like to be truly well cared for.\\n\\n:::warning Important: Rabbit Nutrition\\nRabbits have very specific dietary needs that differ significantly from other small animals. **Never feed a rabbit guinea pig pellets** \u2014 they lack the proper nutrients and can cause serious long-term health problems. Learn more in our [rabbit nutrition guide](/docs/Rabbits/getting-started/basic-care).\\n:::\\n\\nWelcome home, Mr. Buttons. You are going to be just fine. \u{1F430}\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"julius-cheeser-forever-home","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/julius-cheeser-forever-home","source":"@site/blog/2026-01-11-julius-cheeser/2026-01-11-julius-cheeser.md","title":"Julius Cheeser: From Storm Drain to Forever Home","description":"Meet Julius Cheeser \u2014 one of our Long Island mice who was found in a storm drain and has come so incredibly far from where he started. He is ready for his forever home, and we want to make sure the right family finds him.","date":"2026-01-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Success Stories","permalink":"/blog/tags/success-stories","description":"Adoption success stories and happy endings"},{"inline":false,"label":"Mice","permalink":"/blog/tags/mice","description":"Mouse updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Adoptions","permalink":"/blog/tags/adoptions","description":"Adoption updates"}],"readingTime":2.09,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jen Dupee","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/jen"},"imageURL":"/img/jen.jpg","key":"jen"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"julius-cheeser-forever-home","title":"Julius Cheeser: From Storm Drain to Forever Home","authors":["jen"],"tags":["success-stories","mice","adoptions"],"image":"./photo1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Meet Mr. Buttons: A 10-Year-Old Rabbit\'s Second Chance","permalink":"/blog/mr-buttons-rabbit-second-chance"},"nextItem":{"title":"Goodbye Carrie: Our Tiny Ancient Robo","permalink":"/blog/goodbye-carrie-robo-hamster"}},"content":"Meet Julius Cheeser \u2014 one of our Long Island mice who was found in a storm drain and has come so incredibly far from where he started. He is ready for his forever home, and we want to make sure the right family finds him.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![Julius Cheeser](photo1.jpg)\\n*Julius Cheeser, the dashing gentleman of our mouse crew.*\\n\\n## Getting to Know Julius\\n\\nSince moving in with his foster mom in Boston, we have learned so much about this little guy:\\n\\n**He is a champion burrower.** Given the right bedding, Julius will construct elaborate tunnel systems that would impress any engineer. Newspaper bedding is his absolute favorite, and he would love to have that in his forever home.\\n\\n**He is a shy wheel user.** We know the wheel is getting used \u2014 there is always evidence in the morning \u2014 but his foster mom has yet to catch him in the act. He manages to get his exercise entirely in secret, which is very on-brand for a mouse.\\n\\n**He is warming up to hands.** Julius is still a little nervous about being handled, but he has been getting more curious and bolder every week. Fingers are especially interesting to him, probably because fingers bring food. Priorities.\\n\\n**He is seed-obsessed.** Julius will begrudgingly eat his rodent block, but seeds are his true love. He will eat the block, but his enthusiasm is noticeably different.\\n\\n**He is a happy, energetic little dude.** When Julius is in a good mood, he popcorns around his cage with pure joy. It is one of the most delightful things to witness.\\n\\n![Julius exploring](photo2.jpg)\\n*Julius investigating his world.*\\n\\n## His Journey\\n\\nJulius came to us as part of a group of mice found in a storm drain on Long Island. The circumstances were not ideal, but he has made extraordinary progress. He went from a terrified, defensive little mouse to a curious, increasingly trusting companion who is ready to take the next step into his forever home.\\n\\nWe are looking for someone patient and experienced with mice \u2014 someone who will give Julius the time and space he needs to fully come out of his shell. He will reward that patience tenfold.\\n\\n:::tip Learn More About Mice\\nIf you are considering adopting a mouse, check out our [mouse care resources](/docs/Mice/getting-started/mouse-basic-care) for information on housing, enrichment, and what makes mice such wonderful companions.\\n:::\\n\\nIf Julius sounds like the right fit for your home, please reach out to us! \u{1F49B}\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"goodbye-carrie-robo-hamster","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/goodbye-carrie-robo-hamster","source":"@site/blog/2026-01-08-carrie/2026-01-08-carrie.md","title":"Goodbye Carrie: Our Tiny Ancient Robo","description":"We are heartbroken to share that our resident ancient roborovski hamster, Carrie, passed away this week. She was not a spring chicken when she was surrendered to us, and she would have been with us for a full year on January 25th. She kept her energy and her sass right until the very end.","date":"2026-01-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Rainbow Bridge","permalink":"/blog/tags/rainbowbridge","description":"Remembering the pets who have crossed the rainbow bridge."},{"inline":false,"label":"Hamsters","permalink":"/blog/tags/hamsters","description":"Hamster updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"}],"readingTime":1.34,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"goodbye-carrie-robo-hamster","title":"Goodbye Carrie: Our Tiny Ancient Robo","authors":["danni"],"tags":["rainbowbridge","hamsters","sanctuary"],"image":"./photo1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Julius Cheeser: From Storm Drain to Forever Home","permalink":"/blog/julius-cheeser-forever-home"},"nextItem":{"title":"Umbreon & Espeon Find Their Forever Home","permalink":"/blog/umbreon-espeon-adoption-2026"}},"content":"We are heartbroken to share that our resident ancient roborovski hamster, Carrie, passed away this week. She was not a spring chicken when she was surrendered to us, and she would have been with us for a full year on January 25th. She kept her energy and her sass right until the very end.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![Carrie](photo1.jpg)\\n*Carrie in her beloved 40-gallon tank \u2014 the tiny queen of a very large kingdom.*\\n\\n## A Tiny Life, Fully Lived\\n\\nCarrie was a whopping 21 grams. She had an entire 40-gallon tank all to herself, and honestly, it was sometimes very easy to lose her in there. She loved her wheel, she loved exploring every inch of her space, and she had an attitude that was absolutely disproportionate to her size.\\n\\nRoborovski hamsters are the smallest of the hamster species, and they are known for their speed and energy. Carrie was no exception. Even in her final weeks, she was still zooming around and doing things entirely on her own terms.\\n\\n## Sanctuary Life for Senior Hamsters\\n\\nCarrie is a perfect example of why we take in senior and hospice animals. She came to us at an age when many rescues would have passed, and she got to spend her final year in a safe, enriched environment where she was loved and cared for every single day. That matters.\\n\\n:::tip Learn More\\nInterested in hamster care and what makes a great hamster habitat? Check out our [hamster care resources](/docs/Hamsters/getting-started/hamster-basic-care) for information on housing, enrichment, and more.\\n:::\\n\\nTill we meet again, tiny friend. \u{1F439}\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"umbreon-espeon-adoption-2026","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/umbreon-espeon-adoption-2026","source":"@site/blog/2026-01-05-umbreon-espeon/2026-01-05-umbreon-espeon.md","title":"Umbreon & Espeon Find Their Forever Home","description":"We are kicking off 2026 with our very first adoption of the year \u2014 and it\'s a double! Mama Umbreon and her daughter Espeon have gone home, and we couldn\'t be more thrilled about where they landed.","date":"2026-01-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Adoptions","permalink":"/blog/tags/adoptions","description":"Adoption updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Success Stories","permalink":"/blog/tags/success-stories","description":"Adoption success stories and happy endings"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":1.12,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"umbreon-espeon-adoption-2026","title":"Umbreon & Espeon Find Their Forever Home","authors":["danni"],"tags":["adoptions","success-stories","guineapigs"],"image":"./photo1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Goodbye Carrie: Our Tiny Ancient Robo","permalink":"/blog/goodbye-carrie-robo-hamster"},"nextItem":{"title":"Calliope\'s Story: When Ringworm Isn\'t Just Ringworm","permalink":"/blog/calliopes-story-ringworm-staph"}},"content":"We are kicking off 2026 with our very first adoption of the year \u2014 and it\'s a double! Mama Umbreon and her daughter Espeon have gone home, and we couldn\'t be more thrilled about where they landed.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![Umbreon and Espeon](photo1.jpg)\\n*Umbreon and Espeon, heading to their forever home.*\\n\\n## A Perfect Match\\n\\nUmbreon and Espeon went home with one of our fantastic previous adopters \u2014 someone who had already proven themselves to be a wonderful guinea pig parent. They had recently lost one of their guinea pigs and were looking to expand their herd again.\\n\\nTheir plan is to bond Umbreon and Espeon with their two current guinea pigs, giving everyone a chance to become one big happy family. They are also completely comfortable keeping them separate if Umbreon \u2014 who can be a little grumpy with other pigs sometimes \u2014 decides she\'s not interested in making new friends. That kind of flexibility and patience is exactly what makes a great adopter.\\n\\n## Our First Foster Fail of the Year Too!\\n\\nThis adoption came alongside our first foster fail of 2026 as well, making for a very happy start to the new year. Between the foster fail and this adoption, we are already off to a wonderful start.\\n\\nHappy trails, Umbreon and Espeon. We can\'t wait to hear about all your adventures in your new home. \u{1F49B}\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"calliopes-story-ringworm-staph","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/calliopes-story-ringworm-staph","source":"@site/blog/2026-01-04-calliope/2026-01-04-calliope.md","title":"Calliope\'s Story: When Ringworm Isn\'t Just Ringworm","description":"This is Calliope\'s story. It is a story about a skin condition that started small, was mismanaged before she came to us, and ultimately became something far more serious. We are sharing it because we believe her experience can help other guinea pig owners recognize warning signs and advocate for their animals before things escalate.","date":"2026-01-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"},{"inline":false,"label":"Educational","permalink":"/blog/tags/educational","description":"Educational content"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Rainbow Bridge","permalink":"/blog/tags/rainbowbridge","description":"Remembering the pets who have crossed the rainbow bridge."}],"readingTime":2.48,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"calliopes-story-ringworm-staph","title":"Calliope\'s Story: When Ringworm Isn\'t Just Ringworm","authors":["danni"],"tags":["medical","educational","guineapigs","rainbowbridge"],"image":"./photo1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Umbreon & Espeon Find Their Forever Home","permalink":"/blog/umbreon-espeon-adoption-2026"},"nextItem":{"title":"Foster Fail! Jen Keeps Her Ratties","permalink":"/blog/2026-foster-fail-jen-ratties"}},"content":"This is Calliope\'s story. It is a story about a skin condition that started small, was mismanaged before she came to us, and ultimately became something far more serious. We are sharing it because we believe her experience can help other guinea pig owners recognize warning signs and advocate for their animals before things escalate.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![Calliope](photo1.jpg)\\n*Calliope, our sweet girl.*\\n\\n## How It Started\\n\\nCalliope came to us with what appeared to be ringworm \u2014 a fungal infection (dermatophytosis) that is common in guinea pigs. Ringworm in guinea pigs typically presents as circular patches of hair loss, often with scaly or crusty skin at the edges. When caught early, it is very treatable with antifungal medication.\\n\\nThe problem is that ringworm is often misidentified, and treatment is sometimes delayed or incomplete. In Calliope\'s case, the infection had been left untreated long enough that it had progressed well beyond a simple fungal issue.\\n\\n## When Ringworm Becomes Something Worse\\n\\nWhat we discovered was that the original fungal infection had created an entry point for bacteria. The wound had become a complicated, inflamed **staph infection with vasculitis** \u2014 a serious condition involving inflammation of the blood vessels in the skin.\\n\\n![Calliope\'s skin condition](photo2.jpg)\\n*The extent of the infection when she arrived.*\\n\\nVasculitis in guinea pigs is not common, and it is not easy to manage. While we were never able to fully cure it, we were able to keep it well-managed with a combination of treatments. Calliope hated oral medications and topical treatments, so after each treatment session, we would give her a gentle back massage \u2014 medium pressure, which she loved. She would kick her little feet out in contentment, and those moments made every difficult treatment session worth it.\\n\\n## The Lesson: Early Treatment Matters\\n\\nRingworm in guinea pigs is very treatable when caught early. A simple fungal infection that gets trimmed at the follicle and treated immediately is an easy fix. Left untreated, it can become a complicated, multi-layered infection that is much harder to resolve.\\n\\n![Calliope resting](photo3.jpg)\\n*Calliope in one of her favorite spots.*\\n\\n**Signs of ringworm in guinea pigs to watch for:**\\n- Circular patches of hair loss, especially around the face, ears, and back\\n- Scaly, flaky, or crusty skin at the edges of the bald patches\\n- Redness or irritation in the affected area\\n- Scratching or rubbing at the area\\n\\nIf you notice any of these signs, please contact an exotic-knowledgeable veterinarian promptly. Do not wait to see if it resolves on its own.\\n\\n:::tip Check Our Resources\\nWe have a comprehensive guide on [skin conditions and parasites in guinea pigs](/docs/guinea-pigs/illnesses-and-conditions/parasites-in-guinea-pigs) that covers common skin issues and when to seek veterinary care.\\n:::\\n\\n## Goodbye, Calliope\\n\\nWe are so sorry we couldn\'t completely fix you, sweet girl. But you were loved every single day you were with us, and now you are whole and well and popcorning over the rainbow bridge. \u{1F308}\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"2026-foster-fail-jen-ratties","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-foster-fail-jen-ratties","source":"@site/blog/2026-01-01-foster-fail/2026-01-01-foster-fail.md","title":"Foster Fail! Jen Keeps Her Ratties","description":"We are barely 24 hours into 2026 and we already have our first foster fail of the year \u2014 and honestly, we couldn\'t be happier about it.","date":"2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Success Stories","permalink":"/blog/tags/success-stories","description":"Adoption success stories and happy endings"},{"inline":false,"label":"Rats","permalink":"/blog/tags/rats","description":"Rat updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"}],"readingTime":1.26,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-foster-fail-jen-ratties","title":"Foster Fail! Jen Keeps Her Ratties","authors":["danni"],"tags":["success-stories","rats","sanctuary"],"image":"./photo1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Calliope\'s Story: When Ringworm Isn\'t Just Ringworm","permalink":"/blog/calliopes-story-ringworm-staph"},"nextItem":{"title":"2025 Year in Review: 110 Animals, Infinite Love","permalink":"/blog/2025-year-in-review"}},"content":"We are barely 24 hours into 2026 and we already have our first foster fail of the year \u2014 and honestly, we couldn\'t be happier about it.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nJen has decided to keep her foster rats. After losing several of her senior rats last year, and given that these boys have some unique personality quirks that make them a little harder to place, Jen made the call to give them a permanent home. We wholeheartedly support her decision.\\n\\n![Jen\'s new permanent ratties](photo1.jpg)\\n*The boys who decided they were home already.*\\n\\nA \\"foster fail\\" in rescue is actually a success story \u2014 it means a foster parent fell so in love with their foster animal that they couldn\'t bear to let them go. For animals with special needs or shy personalities, a foster fail is often the very best outcome.\\n\\n<div className=\\"row\\" style={{display: \'flex\', gap: \'1rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1.5rem\'}}>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'200px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo2.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Rat portrait\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'200px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo3.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Rat portrait\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div style={{flex: \'1\', minWidth: \'200px\'}}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./photo4.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Rat portrait\\" style={{width: \'100%\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\nThese boys are settling in beautifully with Jen and will be well loved for the rest of their days. Welcome to your forever home, little guys. \u{1F400}\\n\\n![The whole crew](photo5.jpg)\\n*All five boys, officially home.*\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"2025-year-in-review","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2025-year-in-review","source":"@site/blog/2026-01-01-year-in-review/2026-01-01-year-in-review.md","title":"2025 Year in Review: 110 Animals, Infinite Love","description":"What a year it has been. As we step into 2026, we want to take a moment to reflect on everything we accomplished together \u2014 and to thank every single person who made it possible.","date":"2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"News","permalink":"/blog/tags/news","description":"News and announcements"},{"inline":false,"label":"Educational","permalink":"/blog/tags/educational","description":"Educational content"}],"readingTime":2.9,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2025-year-in-review","title":"2025 Year in Review: 110 Animals, Infinite Love","authors":["danni"],"tags":["news","educational"],"image":"./photo1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Foster Fail! Jen Keeps Her Ratties","permalink":"/blog/2026-foster-fail-jen-ratties"},"nextItem":{"title":"How to See More of Our Posts & Help Boost Us!","permalink":"/blog/how-to-follow"}},"content":"What a year it has been. As we step into 2026, we want to take a moment to reflect on everything we accomplished together \u2014 and to thank every single person who made it possible.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![The HALT team reflecting on an incredible year](photo1.jpg)\\n*Gratitude, growth, and a whole lot of tiny paws.*\\n\\n## Our Numbers Tell a Story\\n\\nOur 2025 numbers look a little different from previous years, and that\'s intentional. We made a deliberate shift toward **specialized care for senior, hospice, and special needs small animals** \u2014 the ones who are hardest to place and most in need of experienced hands.\\n\\nEven with that transition, we were able to:\\n\\n- **Place 110 animals** into loving forever homes\\n- Provide **sanctuary care for 95 senior, hospice, and special needs animals** across all of our board members and experienced foster homes\\n\\nThose 95 sanctuary animals represent countless sleepless nights, emergency vet runs, hand-feedings, and moments of pure joy watching a once-suffering animal thrive on a carefully managed care plan.\\n\\n## A Year of Medical Firsts\\n\\n2025 was genuinely one of the most medically complex years we have ever experienced. We encountered conditions we had never seen before, worked with veterinary specialists pushing the boundaries of exotic animal medicine, and came out the other side with a depth of knowledge that will directly benefit every animal we care for going forward.\\n\\nWe saw firsthand how veterinary science has advanced \u2014 new treatment protocols, new medications being applied to exotic species, and a growing community of vets who take small animals as seriously as dogs and cats. Our philosophy has always been: **there is no cap on knowledge**. The best thing we can do for our animal friends is to keep an open mind as science evolves, follow the research, and advocate relentlessly.\\n\\n## A Special Thank-You to Dr. Ford\\n\\nWe want to send an extra-special shout-out to **Dr. Ford at Southern Maine Hospital for Small Mammals** and his incredible team. His advanced knowledge of exotic mammal medicine has helped us provide a quality of care that many thought impossible for animals this small. So many of our sanctuary residents are thriving on their treatment plans, and seeing them happy and comfortable has brought a new level of joy to everyone on our team.\\n\\nIf you are in the New England area and need an exotic small mammal specialist, we cannot recommend Dr. Ford highly enough.\\n\\n## Thank You to Our Village\\n\\nNone of this happens without you. To our **adopters, fosters, supporters, friends, and families** \u2014 your unwavering confidence in the work we do means everything. You show up for us in so many ways: sharing our posts, donating supplies, sponsoring vet visits, and simply cheering us on when things get hard.\\n\\nTo our rescue board members \u2014 **Jen, Alanna, Lynne, and Ericka** \u2014 I am in awe of you every single day. You roll with every punch, say yes to every crazy idea, and show up for each other and for these animals without hesitation. I could not imagine a better team.\\n\\nAnd to our rescue friends across the US and around the globe: the group chats, the shared knowledge, the late-night check-ins on each other\'s mental health \u2014 teamwork truly makes the dreamwork. This community is something special.\\n\\n## Looking Ahead\\n\\nHere\'s to 2026 being another year full of learning, growth, and happy endings \u2014 hopefully with a little less stress along the way. \u{1F49B}\\n\\nWe are so grateful for every one of you.\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"how-to-follow","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/how-to-follow","source":"@site/blog/2025-09-02-follow/2025-09-02-follow.md","title":"How to See More of Our Posts & Help Boost Us!","description":"You\'ve probably noticed that social media, especially Facebook, has been frustrating lately - it\'s hard to see the content you\'re looking for. This has a really negative effect on non-profits who depend on reaching their audience on social media! We\'ve put together a list of ways you can help us and ensure you and others see our content.","date":"2025-09-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"News","permalink":"/blog/tags/news","description":"News and announcements"}],"readingTime":2.12,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Alanna Burke","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/alanna"},"imageURL":"/img/alanna.jpg","key":"alanna"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"how-to-follow","title":"How to See More of Our Posts & Help Boost Us!","authors":["alanna"],"tags":["news"],"image":"./facebook.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"2025 Year in Review: 110 Animals, Infinite Love","permalink":"/blog/2025-year-in-review"},"nextItem":{"title":"Chinchilla Intake","permalink":"/blog/7-2-chinnies"}},"content":"You\'ve probably noticed that social media, especially Facebook, has been frustrating lately - it\'s hard to see the content you\'re looking for. This has a really negative effect on non-profits who depend on reaching their audience on social media! We\'ve put together a list of ways you can help us and ensure you and others see our content. \\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nFacebook and other platforms don\u2019t always show you everything from the pages you follow. Here\u2019s how you can make sure you don\u2019t miss updates from us \u2014 and help us reach more people:\\n\\n### Facebook\\n\\n1. Follow our Page \u2013 click the Follow button if you haven\u2019t already. If you \\"liked\\" our page many years ago, check and make sure that\'s been updated to \\"following,\\" which has now taken the place of \\"like.\\"\\n2. Add us to Favorites \u2013 tap the \u201CFollowing\u201D dropdown and select Favorites. This tells Facebook to show you our posts higher in your feed.\\n3. Engage with our posts \u2013 every like, comment, share, or click signals to Facebook that people want to see more of our content. The love, care, and wow reactions seem to help the most. And don\'t worry about making a clever comment - just comment 4 or more words! \\"Wow, they\'re so cute!\\" will do. \\n\\n![facebook](facebook.jpg)\\n\\n![react](react.png)\\n\\n### Instagram\\n\\n - Tap the Follow button.\\n - Go to our profile \u2192 tap the bell icon \u2192 Turn on notifications for posts & stories.\\n - Like, share to your stories, or comment to help us show up in more feeds.\\n\\n![insta](insta.jpg)\\n\\n![insta](insta2.jpg)\\n\\n![insta](insta3.jpg)\\n\\n### Threads\\n\\n - Tap the Follow button.\\n - Go to our profile \u2192 tap the bell icon \u2192 Turn on notifications for posts & stories.\\n - Like, share to your stories, or comment to help us show up in more feeds.\\n\\n![threads](threads.jpg)\\n\\n![threads](threads2.jpg)\\n\\n![threads](threads3.jpg)\\n\\n### Mastodon\\n\\n - Follow us to see updates.\\n - Like and reply to our content - engagement helps!\\n - Boost and repost our content \u2014 this is one of the strongest ways to spread the word.\\n\\n![mastodon](mastodon.jpg)\\n\\n### Bluesky\\n\\n - Follow us to see updates.\\n - Like and reply to our content - engagement helps!\\n - Boost and repost our content \u2014 this is one of the strongest ways to spread the word.\\n\\n![Blue Sky](bsky1.jpg)\\n\\n![Blue Sky](bsky2.jpg)\\n\\n![Blue Sky](bsky3.jpg)\\n\\n### Why This Matters\\n\\nSocial media platforms show our posts to more people when you interact with them. Following, favoriting, liking, sharing, or even leaving a quick emoji comment makes a big difference.\\n\\nIt\u2019s a free and easy way to support our work and make sure the animals in our care get seen and supported.\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"7-2-chinnies","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/7-2-chinnies","source":"@site/blog/2025-07-2-chinnies/2025-07-2-chinnies.md","title":"Chinchilla Intake","description":"Our first surrender of the day is a trio of chinchillas\u2014how cute are these ladies?! \u{1F63B}","date":"2025-07-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Chinchillas","permalink":"/blog/tags/chinchillas","description":"Chinchilla updates and stories"}],"readingTime":1.07,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jen Dupee","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/jen"},"imageURL":"/img/jen.jpg","key":"jen"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"7-2-chinnies","title":"Chinchilla Intake","authors":["jen"],"tags":["chinchillas"],"image":"./chinnies7-2.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to See More of Our Posts & Help Boost Us!","permalink":"/blog/how-to-follow"},"nextItem":{"title":"Adoption Alert - Ziggy","permalink":"/blog/7-2-ziggy"}},"content":"Our first surrender of the day is a trio of chinchillas\u2014how cute are these ladies?! \u{1F63B}\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThey also come with one of the funnier surrender stories we\u2019ve heard in a while\u2026\\n\\nThe person who surrendered them wasn\u2019t actually expecting to have chinchillas at all. Her significant other is a contractor and was doing work in someone\u2019s home. As he was leaving, the homeowner asked, \u201CDo you want a chinchilla?\u201D\\n\\nHe\u2014being a perfectly reasonable person\u2014replied, \u201CNo, I don\u2019t know anything about chinchillas!\u201D\\n\\nBut she persisted.\\n\u201CHow about you take all three?\u201D\\nSo\u2026 off he went, with three chinchillas in tow. \u{1F602}\\n\\nHis partner was amused, but understandably overwhelmed. She explained that she had no experience with chinchillas and didn\u2019t feel equipped to care for three surprise floofs. They genuinely wanted the best for the girls and reached out to us\u2014and of course, we said yes!\\n\\nAll three are girls (yay!) and one of them is especially adorable and\u2026 a little extra round\u2014she\u2019s practically rolling. \u{1F602} We\u2019ll make sure she gets on a healthy track, and they\u2019ll all receive the care, space, and one-on-one attention they deserve.\\n\\nEven better? They\u2019ll be living permanently with one of our amazing board members! \u{1F496}\\n\\n![Chinnies](chinnies7-2.jpg)\\n![Chinnies](chinnies7-2-1.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"7-2-ziggy","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/7-2-ziggy","source":"@site/blog/2025-07-2-ziggy/2025-07-2-ziggy.md","title":"Adoption Alert - Ziggy","description":"Alanna has officially welcomed Ziggy\u2014Hero\u2019s \u201Cnormal\u201D brother\u2014into her home!","date":"2025-07-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Adoptions","permalink":"/blog/tags/adoptions","description":"Adoption updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.51,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"7-2-ziggy","title":"Adoption Alert - Ziggy","authors":["danni"],"tags":["adoptions","guineapigs"],"image":"./ziggy.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Chinchilla Intake","permalink":"/blog/7-2-chinnies"},"nextItem":{"title":"Baby Mice Growing Up!","permalink":"/blog/7-1-mice"}},"content":"Alanna has officially welcomed Ziggy\u2014Hero\u2019s \u201Cnormal\u201D brother\u2014into her home!\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nHe had just reached the age where I was about to list him on Petfinder\u2026 but Alanna snatched him up before anyone else had the chance. \u{1F604}\\n\\nZiggy will be living the good life with two HALT alumni boys, and let\u2019s be honest\u2014he\u2019s going to be extra spoiled. And since Alanna and I talk every day, I fully expect to receive daily photo updates. \u{1F609}\\n\\nWe\u2019re so excited for this little guy and so grateful he\u2019s staying close to the HALT family! \u{1F49B}\\n\\n![Ziggy](ziggy.jpg)\\n![Ziggy](ziggy2.jpg)\\n![Ziggy](ziggy3.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"7-1-mice","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/7-1-mice","source":"@site/blog/2025-07-1-mice/2025-07-1-mice.md","title":"Baby Mice Growing Up!","description":"Remember the baby mice who came in at just a week old without a mom?","date":"2025-07-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Mice","permalink":"/blog/tags/mice","description":"Mouse updates and stories"}],"readingTime":1.47,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jen Dupee","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/jen"},"imageURL":"/img/jen.jpg","key":"jen"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"7-1-mice","title":"Baby Mice Growing Up!","authors":["jen"],"tags":["mice"],"image":"./mice.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Adoption Alert - Ziggy","permalink":"/blog/7-2-ziggy"},"nextItem":{"title":"More Vet Updates","permalink":"/blog/7-1-vet-update"}},"content":"Remember the baby mice who came in at just a week old without a mom?\\nWell\u2026 they\u2019ve grown SO much, they\u2019re almost unrecognizable now! \u{1F63B}\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThese adorable little guys and gals are fully eating solid food and doing remarkably well, all things considered. They\u2019re still a bit smaller than we\u2019d like for their age, but that\u2019s pretty common in hand-reared babies. We\u2019ll be holding most of them back for at least another month to give them time to catch up in weight and continue thriving\u2014but we\u2019re hopeful that many will be ready for adoption soon! \u{1F970}\\n\\nNow I know what you\u2019re all thinking:\\n\u201CBut what about names?\u201D\\nBecause clearly, I have such a great track record with names\u2026 \u{1F974}\\n\\nWell, Finding Nemo has been on my sleep-deprived mind lately, and it felt like a perfect theme. Except, of course, I couldn\u2019t stick with normal names. So, I\u2019m very proud (and slightly unhinged) to introduce you to:\\n\\n\u{1F420} P. Sherman, 42 Wallaby Way, Sydney    \\n\u{1F41F} Just Keep Swimming    \\n\u{1FAE7} I Shall Call Him Squishy    \\n\u{1F374} Fish Are Friends, Not Food    \\n\u{1F988} Shark Bait, Hoo Ha Ha    \\n\u{1F6AB} I\u2019m Going to Touch the Butt    \\n\u{1F5E3} Mine Mine Mine    \\n\\nAt some point, Danni is definitely going to stop letting me name things \u{1F602}\\n(My other brilliant idea today was to name our satin guinea pigs after Twilight vampires because they sparkle\u2014\u201CThis is the fur of a killer, Bella.\u201D \u{1F605})\\n\\nAnyway, this turned into quite a ramble\u2014but we hope you enjoyed meeting the not-so-little-anymore babies!\\nAs always, if you\u2019re able to support our work, donations are greatly appreciated. \u{1F49B}\\n\\n![Baby Mice](mice.jpg)\\n![Baby Mice](mice2.jpg)\\n![Baby Mice](mice3.jpg)\\n![Baby Mice](mice4.jpg)\\n![Baby Mice](mice5.jpg)\\n![Baby Mice](mice6.jpg)\\n![Baby Mice](mice7.jpg)\\n![Baby Mice](mice8.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"7-1-vet-update","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/7-1-vet-update","source":"@site/blog/2025-07-1-vet-update/2025-07-1-vet-update.md","title":"More Vet Updates","description":"Just a few more updates from yesterday\u2019s vet visit\u2014nothing too exciting, but still worth sharing!","date":"2025-07-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":1.01,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"7-1-vet-update","title":"More Vet Updates","authors":["danni"],"tags":["sanctuary","medical","guineapigs"],"image":"./7-1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Baby Mice Growing Up!","permalink":"/blog/7-1-mice"},"nextItem":{"title":"1 AM Fun!","permalink":"/blog/6-30-blowdry"}},"content":"Just a few more updates from yesterday\u2019s vet visit\u2014nothing too exciting, but still worth sharing!\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n\\n\u{1F439} Sonnet was originally my parents\u2019 foster piggie. She had a very wonky uterus and ovarian cysts, which were removed before she came up here to be a companion for Old Man Colin (who just doesn\u2019t do well with other boys).\\nAt her check-up, we found that one of her stitches hadn\u2019t absorbed properly and had gotten a tiny bit infected. It was removed, and it already looks so much better.\\n\\nAlso\u2014she is so itty bitty for her age! She\u2019s a year and a half old, but she\u2019s maybe the size of a 3\u20134 month old baby.\\n\\n\u{1F437} Lillia is one of Maddy\u2019s bonded friends. She\u2019s definitely on the older side\u2014around six years old\u2014and very arthritic. But oh my goodness, what a love bug she is! She adores sprawling out and getting chin rubs. Total sweetheart.\\n\\n\u{1F43D} Marlow came to us as a private owner surrender. She\u2019s also a senior lady, but here\u2019s the exciting part: she got a clean bill of health! And honestly? That\u2019s thrilling to me. \u{1F602}\\n\\n![Sonnet](7-1.jpg)\\n![Lillia](7-1-2.jpg)\\n![Marlow](7-1-3.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-30-blowdry","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-30-blowdry","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-30-blowdry/2025-06-30-blowdry.md","title":"1 AM Fun!","description":"What is Jen doing at 1am?","date":"2025-06-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Humans","permalink":"/blog/tags/humans","description":"Posts about our volunteers and team"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.2,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jen Dupee","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/jen"},"imageURL":"/img/jen.jpg","key":"jen"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-30-blowdry","title":"1 AM Fun!","authors":["jen"],"tags":["humans","guineapigs"],"image":"./blowdry.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"More Vet Updates","permalink":"/blog/7-1-vet-update"},"nextItem":{"title":"Hero Update - Heart Disease","permalink":"/blog/6-30-hero"}},"content":"What is Jen doing at 1am?\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nWhy blow drying guinea pig butts of course! \u{1F974}\\nThat\'s all, it\'s late and I just felt everyone needed to know \u{1F602}\\n\\n![Spa time](blowdry.jpg)\\n![Spa time](blowdry2.jpg)\\n![Spa time](blowdry3.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-30-hero","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-30-hero","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-30-hero/2025-06-30-hero.md","title":"Hero Update - Heart Disease","description":"I brought several piggies to Southern Maine Hospital for Small Mammals today, and I know I owe everyone updates on both the new arrivals and those who were seen\u2014but I want to start with Hero\u2019s appointment.","date":"2025-06-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Lethal White","permalink":"/blog/tags/lethalwhite","description":"Lethal White guinea pigs"},{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.93,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-30-hero","title":"Hero Update - Heart Disease","authors":["danni"],"tags":["sanctuary","lethalwhite","medical","guineapigs"],"image":"./hero630.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"1 AM Fun!","permalink":"/blog/6-30-blowdry"},"nextItem":{"title":"Maddy Update","permalink":"/blog/6-30-maddy"}},"content":"I brought several piggies to Southern Maine Hospital for Small Mammals today, and I know I owe everyone updates on both the new arrivals and those who were seen\u2014but I want to start with Hero\u2019s appointment.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThe good news: Hero is doing significantly better on Lasix.\\n\\nWe did an ultrasound of his heart, and it turns out that his likely birth defect is muscular\u2014one side of his heart beats harder than the other, which caused some fluid to build up and leak.\\n\\nThe even better news?\\n\\nBecause it\u2019s a muscular defect (not valve-related), it means he may live much longer than we originally expected when he was first diagnosed with heart disease. \u{1F49B}\\n\\nHero will be on Lasix for the rest of his life, but it\u2019s already made a huge difference in his quality of life. He\u2019s popcorning again, which is incredible to see\u2014but he does tire more easily and takes frequent naps. And honestly? That\u2019s okay. He\u2019s still the happiest little pig, and I\u2019m so grateful we caught this early enough to help him feel better.\\n\\n![Hero\'s Ultrasound](hero630.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-30-maddy","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-30-maddy","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-30-maddy/2025-06-30-maddy.md","title":"Maddy Update","description":"Some optimistic news about Maddy! \u{1F49B}","date":"2025-06-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.99,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-30-maddy","title":"Maddy Update","authors":["danni"],"tags":["sanctuary","medical","guineapigs"],"image":"./maddy630.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Hero Update - Heart Disease","permalink":"/blog/6-30-hero"},"nextItem":{"title":"New Arrivals in NH!","permalink":"/blog/6-29-nh-intakes"}},"content":"Some optimistic news about Maddy! \u{1F49B}\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThe tumor in her ear isn\u2019t too deep into the canal, and our vet is very hopeful about the outcome of surgical removal.\\n\\nWe actually did an ultrasound of her ear\u2014which I\u2019ve never done before\u2014and it was pretty cool to see! That sort of oblong shape on the screen is the tumor. I got a photo of that view, though I missed the one showing the ear canal itself.\\n\\nMaddy is scheduled for surgery on July 10th, with an estimated cost of $406.\\nI decided against scheduling it right before the holiday weekend\u2014just in case there were complications, I wanted to ensure full access to post-op care.\\n\\nI do have a full cost breakdown in the rescue room, but I\u2019m going to be honest\u2014I feel like hot garbage right now. I\u2019m going to rest a bit, watch the ballgame, and maybe doze off for half an hour before jumping back into everything.\\n\\nIn the meantime, we urgently need help covering:\\n - Today\u2019s appointment for five piggies, which came to over $300\\n - Maddy\u2019s upcoming surgery, quoted at $400+\\n\\n![Maddy](maddy630.jpg)\\n![Maddy](maddy6301.jpg)\\n![Maddy](maddy6302.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-29-nh-intakes","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-29-nh-intakes","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-29-nh-intakes/2025-06-29-nj-intakes.md","title":"New Arrivals in NH!","description":"SURPRISE!","date":"2025-06-29T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"News","permalink":"/blog/tags/news","description":"News and announcements"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":1.22,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-29-nh-intakes","title":"New Arrivals in NH!","authors":["danni"],"tags":["sanctuary","news","guineapigs"],"image":"./maddy.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Maddy Update","permalink":"/blog/6-30-maddy"},"nextItem":{"title":"A Sad Update on Quintan","permalink":"/blog/6-28-quintan"}},"content":"SURPRISE!\\nMaddy has a tumor in her ear. \u{1F61E}\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nI first saw her\u2014and her bonded cagemates\u2014on our friends\u2019 page at Judge\u2019s Park. I was immediately drawn to Maddy because she reminded me so much of a very special pig from my past, Maddox. Then I saw that they were all listed as six years old, and I thought, you know what? They can come here. I love little old ladies.\\n\\nThey arrived today, and during my intake checks, I found the tumor\u2014and my heart dropped.\\n\\nIt needs to come out immediately. Tumors in this location can spread into the ear bulla or even reach the brain if left untreated. It\u2019s difficult to get clean margins in the ear, but we\u2019ve done this before: when Nova had an ear tumor removed, it took years to come back. So I\u2019m definitely willing to give this a go\u2014if we can raise the funds.\\n\\nI\u2019ll be getting a surgical quote tomorrow when I\u2019m up at Southern Maine Hospital for Small Mammals, but we\u2019re estimating around $500 for the procedure.\\n\\nOne note: I\u2019m starting to think Maddy may be younger than her cagemates. They definitely seem older, but Maddy herself might be closer to three years old\u2014which makes the idea of pursuing surgery even more hopeful.\\n\\nIf you\u2019re able to chip in toward Maddy\u2019s urgent surgery, it would mean the world. Let\u2019s give this sweet girl the fighting chance she deserves. \u{1F49B}\\n\\n![Maddy](maddy.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-28-quintan","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-28-quintan","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-28-quintan/2025-06-28-quintan.md","title":"A Sad Update on Quintan","description":"Quintan passed peacefully over the rainbow bridge.","date":"2025-06-28T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Rainbow Bridge","permalink":"/blog/tags/rainbowbridge","description":"Remembering the pets who have crossed the rainbow bridge."},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.38,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-28-quintan","title":"A Sad Update on Quintan","authors":["danni"],"tags":["sanctuary","rainbowbridge","guineapigs"],"image":"./quintan.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"New Arrivals in NH!","permalink":"/blog/6-29-nh-intakes"},"nextItem":{"title":"Chinchilla Updates","permalink":"/blog/6-27-chinnies"}},"content":"Quintan passed peacefully over the rainbow bridge.\\nHe drifted off quietly while snuggled up against me.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nI hate that he\u2019s gone\u2014he was such a darling pig with a huge personality. But if an animal has to pass, I always hope it\u2019s peaceful and quick, and I\u2019m grateful that\u2019s how it happened for him.\\n\\nHe will be deeply missed by me and by Team Wonkiest Boys.\\nPopcorn free, my little friend. \u{1F49B}\\n\\n![quintan](quintan.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-27-chinnies","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-27-chinnies","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-27-chinnies/2025-06-27-chinnies.md","title":"Chinchilla Updates","description":"Several days ago, I was very shocked to find that Meredith the chinnie had passed away. She was Ellis\u2019s sister\u2014our chinchilla with congestive heart failure\u2014and a bonded companion to Lexi.","date":"2025-06-27T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Rainbow Bridge","permalink":"/blog/tags/rainbowbridge","description":"Remembering the pets who have crossed the rainbow bridge."},{"inline":false,"label":"Chinchillas","permalink":"/blog/tags/chinchillas","description":"Chinchilla updates and stories"}],"readingTime":1.38,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-27-chinnies","title":"Chinchilla Updates","authors":["danni"],"tags":["sanctuary","rainbowbridge","chinchillas"],"image":"./chinnies.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"A Sad Update on Quintan","permalink":"/blog/6-28-quintan"},"nextItem":{"title":"Difficult Hero Update","permalink":"/blog/6-25-hero-update"}},"content":"Several days ago, I was very shocked to find that Meredith the chinnie had passed away. She was Ellis\u2019s sister\u2014our chinchilla with congestive heart failure\u2014and a bonded companion to Lexi.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nI spoke with our vet, and he explained that Meredith may have also had heart disease, even without showing symptoms. Sadly, heart disease is becoming much more common in chinchillas\u2014so much so that our vet now does chest X-rays before any surgery to make sure their hearts are strong enough for anesthesia.\\n\\nPeople often asked me how I could tell Ellis and Meredith apart, and for me, it was easy:\\nMeredith had more freckles on her ears, and she was always just a bit more human-friendly.\\n\\nOf course, I would never try to replace Meredith\u2014or any animal that passes under my care\u2014but yesterday we received a message about a single female chinchilla in need. Thanks to quick action from one of our rescue friends and Jen, this girl will be coming to us on Sunday with a group of piggies.\\n\\nI\u2019m doing this mostly for Lexi.\\n\\nEllis could pass away at any time, and I don\u2019t want to be scrambling to find a companion for Lexi in the middle of grief. My hope is to bond them as a trio, so Lexi won\u2019t be left alone when the time comes.\\n\\nThis is one of the hardest parts of rescue. Sometimes, even when your human heart isn\u2019t ready, you have to put the emotional needs of herd animals first\u2014because they don\u2019t understand loss the way we do. But they feel it all the same.\\n\\n![Chinchillas](chinnies.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-25-hero-update","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-25-hero-update","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-25-hero-update/2025-06-25-hero-update.md","title":"Difficult Hero Update","description":"Hero is doing better after some oxygen support and a dose of Lasix.","date":"2025-06-25T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Lethal White","permalink":"/blog/tags/lethalwhite","description":"Lethal White guinea pigs"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":1.55,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-25-hero-update","title":"Difficult Hero Update","authors":["danni"],"tags":["sanctuary","lethalwhite","guineapigs"],"image":"./hero625-1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Chinchilla Updates","permalink":"/blog/6-27-chinnies"},"nextItem":{"title":"A Slice of Rescue Life","permalink":"/blog/6-25-rescue-life"}},"content":"Hero is doing better after some oxygen support and a dose of Lasix.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThankfully, I already had Lasix on hand for Alice and Ellis, who are both managing congestive heart failure, so we didn\u2019t need to fill any new prescriptions today.\\n\\nBut\u2026 my heart hurts.\\n\\nI had truly hoped that since Hero is so high-functioning, he might have a chance at a more \u201Cnormal-ish\u201D lifespan. This just doesn\u2019t feel fair. He\u2019s my little shoulder buddy\u2014so full of life and joy\u2014and now he has this to deal with too.\\n\\nHe still wants to be on my shoulder at all times, which gives me comfort, but he tires so easily now.\\nThe hardest part about heart disease is knowing that a cardiac event could happen at any time. The only small mercy is that when it happens, it\u2019s usually fast\u2014too fast for suffering.\\n\\nI\u2019ve been considering investing in an oxygen concentrator for the rescue for years, but until now, we haven\u2019t had a consistent need. That may be changing. We\u2019re seeing a rise in heart disease across multiple species, and now might be the time to seriously pursue it.\\n\\nToday\u2019s vet visit, thankfully, wasn\u2019t as costly as I\u2019d feared\u2014but we still need help covering the expenses. Every little bit helps, especially as Hero begins what may be a long road of management and care.\\n\\nWe also have a follow-up appointment scheduled with Dr. Ford first thing tomorrow morning. Right now, it\u2019s a placeholder in case his breathing worsens overnight. If he\u2019s stable, we can cancel\u2014but if things go downhill, we\u2019ll be ready.\\n\\nThank you, as always, for being in Hero\u2019s corner. \u{1F49B}\\n(And yes, he\u2019s currently curled up on my shoulder, right where he wants to be.)\\n\\n![Hero](hero625-1.jpg)\\n![Hero](hero625-2.jpg)\\n![Hero](hero625-3.jpg)\\n![Hero](hero625-4.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-25-rescue-life","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-25-rescue-life","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-25-rescue-life/2025-06-25-rescue-life.md","title":"A Slice of Rescue Life","description":"It may seem like a small win, but to me it\u2019s a big one:","date":"2025-06-25T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Humans","permalink":"/blog/tags/humans","description":"Posts about our volunteers and team"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.85,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-25-rescue-life","title":"A Slice of Rescue Life","authors":["danni"],"tags":["humans","guineapigs"],"image":"./rescue-life.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Difficult Hero Update","permalink":"/blog/6-25-hero-update"},"nextItem":{"title":"Hero Update","permalink":"/blog/6-24-hero"}},"content":"It may seem like a small win, but to me it\u2019s a big one:\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nNovel has started drinking water from a bottle! \u{1F4A7}\\n\\nThat means I can cut back significantly on giving him sub-q fluids\u2014which is a huge relief for both of us.\\n\\nI don\u2019t usually like sharing photos of myself, but Alanna snapped this one the other night, and honestly, it captures my evenings perfectly. This is my life:\\n\\n- Handfeeding whoever needs it.\\n- Hero on my shoulder.\\n- Sometimes Parody pops out to see what\u2019s going on.\\n- I noodle around on my phone while they chew. The baseball game or music is playing in the background, and if it\u2019s music, I sing softly to them.\\n\\nIt\u2019s not glamorous. It\u2019s not quiet. But it\u2019s ours.\\n\\nI love this little routine, and I treasure every moment\u2014because I know it won\u2019t last forever.\\n\\n(Also, I swear the oral syringe is not poking Novel in the eye like it looks in the photo \u{1F606}\u{1F606})\\n\\n![A Slice of Rescue Life](rescue-life.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-24-hero","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-24-hero","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-24-hero/2025-06-24-hero.md","title":"Hero Update","description":"I\u2019m in a bit of a tizzy\u2014Hero isn\u2019t feeling well. \u{1F614}","date":"2025-06-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Lethal White","permalink":"/blog/tags/lethalwhite","description":"Lethal White guinea pigs"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":1.62,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-24-hero","title":"Hero Update","authors":["danni"],"tags":["sanctuary","lethalwhite","guineapigs"],"image":"./hero625.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"A Slice of Rescue Life","permalink":"/blog/6-25-rescue-life"},"nextItem":{"title":"Novel","permalink":"/blog/6-23-novel"}},"content":"I\u2019m in a bit of a tizzy\u2014Hero isn\u2019t feeling well. \u{1F614}\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nMonday night, he was 10,000% his normal self\u2014playing on my shoulder, jumping on my head, being his usual joyful little chaos gremlin. I had scheduled a vet appointment for Tuesday just to double-check his teeth, and when I packed him into his carrier, everything still seemed completely normal.\\n\\nBut when we arrived at the vet, I noticed his breathing was slightly labored. Dr. Ford listened to his chest and said his lungs sounded junky, so we started him on antibiotics, suspecting pneumonia.\\n\\nAs the day went on, his breathing became more labored, and he even developed a cough. My heart dropped. I started to worry that maybe it wasn\u2019t pneumonia\u2014maybe it was a heart issue. I called every emergency vet in New Hampshire, but not one would see a guinea pig.\\n\\nBoston was my backup plan, but Hero remained alert and relatively active, so I held off. Our primary vet is fully booked today, but thankfully our backup vet was able to squeeze us in. He\u2019s currently on oxygen to help him breathe more comfortably, and we\u2019ll be doing chest X-rays shortly.\\n\\nDr. MacDearmid noted that Hero \u201Clooks like he\u2019s enjoying himself,\u201D because of course\u2014even now\u2014he\u2019s still the happiest pig in the world, no matter what\u2019s going on.\\n\\nHe\u2019s upright, alert, and still active\u2014just not quite as rambunctious as usual.\\nI hate to say I\u2019m hoping for pneumonia, because it can be very serious\u2026 but at least it\u2019s treatable.\\nI have this nagging worry that it could be heart disease, and I hope with everything I\u2019ve got that I\u2019m wrong.\\n\\nThis was another very unexpected vet visit, and while this clinic is amazing and often gives us breaks, it\u2019s still more expensive than our primary vet.\\nIf you\u2019re able, please consider sponsoring his oxygen or X-rays\u2014any little bit helps.\\n\\n![Hero](hero625.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-23-novel","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-23-novel","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-23-novel/2025-06-23-novel.md","title":"Novel","description":"I\u2019m so pleased with how Novel is coming along.","date":"2025-06-23T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.95,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-23-novel","title":"Novel","authors":["danni"],"tags":["sanctuary","guineapigs"],"image":"./novel.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Hero Update","permalink":"/blog/6-24-hero"},"nextItem":{"title":"Memoir","permalink":"/blog/6-21-memoir"}},"content":"I\u2019m so pleased with how Novel is coming along.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n\\nHe\u2019s bright-eyed, more mobile than he\u2019s been in a long time, and really benefiting from pain management and lots of handfeeding. While he probably won\u2019t ever be zooming around the cage, he\u2019s now able to putter around\u2014and most importantly, he doesn\u2019t sit in his urine anymore, which is a huge win for his health and comfort.\\n\\nWe\u2019re up to about 60ml of recovery food per feeding, which is amazing! He takes to handfeeding so well that I\u2019m about to try offering an additional 60ml to see if he\u2019s interested. Now that he\u2019s moving around and eating a bit on his own, my next goal is to get his weight up to a more normal number.\\n\\nI\u2019ll probably be handfeeding him for the rest of his life, considering how worn down his jaw is, but thankfully he makes it so easy.\\n\\nHe\u2019s also rediscovering some favorite foods\u2014he loves spring mix, celery, and will even push through the discomfort for a baby carrot (though I\u2019ve started giving him shredded carrot to make it easier to chew). \u{1F955}\u{1F49B}\\n\\n![Novel](novel.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-21-memoir","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-21-memoir","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-21-memoir/2025-06-21-memoir.md","title":"Memoir","description":"With heavy hearts, we\u2019ve made the decision to bring Memoir in for humane euthanasia today. She\u2019s no longer able to swallow even water, and while we had hoped to wait for our regular vet on Monday, it simply wouldn\u2019t be fair to make her wait and suffer. \u{1F494}","date":"2025-06-21T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Satin Syndrome","permalink":"/blog/tags/satin","description":"Satin syndrome in guinea pigs"},{"inline":false,"label":"Rainbow Bridge","permalink":"/blog/tags/rainbowbridge","description":"Remembering the pets who have crossed the rainbow bridge."},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.52,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-21-memoir","title":"Memoir","authors":["danni"],"tags":["sanctuary","satin","rainbowbridge","guineapigs"],"image":"./memoir.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Novel","permalink":"/blog/6-23-novel"},"nextItem":{"title":"Parody","permalink":"/blog/6-21-parody"}},"content":"With heavy hearts, we\u2019ve made the decision to bring Memoir in for humane euthanasia today. She\u2019s no longer able to swallow even water, and while we had hoped to wait for our regular vet on Monday, it simply wouldn\u2019t be fair to make her wait and suffer. \u{1F494}\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nSince this is a last-minute Saturday appointment, there is an emergency fee involved. If you\u2019re able to contribute, we would be incredibly grateful for any donations to help cover the cost of helping Memoir cross the rainbow bridge peacefully.\\n\\nThank you, as always, for your compassion and support. \u{1F49B}\\n\\n![Memoir](memoir.jpg)\\n![Memoir](memoir2.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-21-parody","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-21-parody","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-21-parody/2025-06-21-parody.md","title":"Parody","description":"Just a little Parody appreciation post. \u{1F49B}","date":"2025-06-21T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.85,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-21-parody","title":"Parody","authors":["danni"],"tags":["sanctuary","guineapigs"],"image":"./parody.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Memoir","permalink":"/blog/6-21-memoir"},"nextItem":{"title":"Haiku Update","permalink":"/blog/6-20-haiku"}},"content":"Just a little Parody appreciation post. \u{1F49B}\\n\\nI really thought I\u2019d have to move Parody to a hospice foster home because of his difficulty getting along with other piggies. And then he got so sick\u2014I honestly didn\u2019t think he\u2019d pull through. That\u2019s when I moved him to the couch cage, and everything changed.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThere, he met Hero, and the two of them absolutely fell in love. Now they\u2019re side by side all the time, inseparable companions.\\n\\nSince I\u2019ve been a bit under the weather this week, I\u2019ve spent more time next to their cage\u2014and Parody has made it his job to check in on me. He\u2019ll poke his little nose at me for a few boops, then wander off to wherever Hero is waiting.\\n\\nHe also likes to pop over during Novel\u2019s handfeeding sessions, just so he can sneak a few milliliters of recovery food\u2014not because he needs it, but because he likes it. And honestly? I\u2019m perfectly okay with that.\\n\\n![Parody](parody.jpg)\\n![Parody](parody2.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-20-haiku","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-20-haiku","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-20-haiku/2025-06-20-haiku.md","title":"Haiku Update","description":"Haiku is truly a character.","date":"2025-06-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":1.11,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-20-haiku","title":"Haiku Update","authors":["danni"],"tags":["sanctuary","medical","guineapigs"],"image":"./haiku.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Parody","permalink":"/blog/6-21-parody"},"nextItem":{"title":"Hero Update","permalink":"/blog/6-20-hero"}},"content":"Haiku is truly a character.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nAt home, she\u2019s the sweetest little thing\u2014she lays on my chest, bruxes contentedly, and covers my face, hands, and arms in kisses. But the moment we step into the vet\u2019s office? She transforms into a feral wild child. \u{1F605}\\n\\nWe recently got all of her test results back, and things are\u2026 a bit weird. Her urine specific gravity was low, which initially pointed us toward kidney issues. But her bloodwork came back completely normal, aside from some mild anemia. We decided to dig deeper and tested her T4 levels to rule out thyroid disease, but those results were normal too.\\n\\nThat said, her urine looked a bit more normal today, which is encouraging. For now, our weekend focus is on getting her weight up\u2014which looks promising since she seems to enjoy the recovery food.\\n\\nNext week, we\u2019ll be following up on a few things with our vet:\\n\\n - Penelope may need another hormone injection\\n - Memoir needs a recheck\u2014I still can\u2019t get her to eat, and I\u2019m worried her satin syndrome is progressing quickly\\n - Hero\u2019s teeth will be discussed\\n - And depending on Haiku\u2019s weight trend, we\u2019ll revisit our options for her too\\n\\nLots going on\u2014but we\u2019re taking it one day (and one pig) at a time. \u{1F49B}\\n\\n![Haiku](haiku.jpg)\\n![Haiku](haiku2.jpg)\\n![Haiku](haiku3.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-20-hero","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-20-hero","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-20-hero/2025-06-20-hero.md","title":"Hero Update","description":"Hero is growing like a weed! \u{1F331} But I do have some concerns about his teeth. I\u2019ve been trimming them once a week\u2014which is thankfully pretty easy\u2014but the color of his top teeth often looks like a sign of scurvy.","date":"2025-06-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Lethal White","permalink":"/blog/tags/lethalwhite","description":"Lethal White guinea pigs"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.55,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-20-hero","title":"Hero Update","authors":["danni"],"tags":["sanctuary","lethalwhite","guineapigs"],"image":"./hero620.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Haiku Update","permalink":"/blog/6-20-haiku"},"nextItem":{"title":"Adoption Alert - Snow White","permalink":"/blog/6-19-snowwhite"}},"content":"Hero is growing like a weed! \u{1F331} But I do have some concerns about his teeth. I\u2019ve been trimming them once a week\u2014which is thankfully pretty easy\u2014but the color of his top teeth often looks like a sign of scurvy.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nHe\u2019s already on a high vitamin C diet and also receives oral vitamin C supplements, so we\u2019ll be checking in with Dr. Ford soon to see what he thinks.\\n\\nIn the meantime, Hero continues to be the most joyful piggie I\u2019ve ever met. He popcorns constantly, and when he\u2019s not mid-bounce, he\u2019s napping curled up with Parody and Novel. \u{1F49B}\\n\\n![Hero](hero620.jpg)\\n![Hero](hero2620.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-19-snowwhite","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-19-snowwhite","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-19-snowwhite/2025-06-19-snowwhite.md","title":"Adoption Alert - Snow White","description":"We are very excited to announce that Snow White has found a wonderful forever home! Congrats pretty girl! You will be so spoiled!","date":"2025-06-19T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Adoptions","permalink":"/blog/tags/adoptions","description":"Adoption updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.14,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jen Dupee","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/jen"},"imageURL":"/img/jen.jpg","key":"jen"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-19-snowwhite","title":"Adoption Alert - Snow White","authors":["jen"],"tags":["adoptions","guineapigs"],"image":"./snowwhite.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Hero Update","permalink":"/blog/6-20-hero"},"nextItem":{"title":"Wheelbarrow","permalink":"/blog/6-19-wheelbarrow"}},"content":"We are very excited to announce that Snow White has found a wonderful forever home! Congrats pretty girl! You will be so spoiled!\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![Snow White](snowwhite.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-19-wheelbarrow","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-19-wheelbarrow","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-19-wheelbarrow/2025-06-19-wheelbarrow.md","title":"Wheelbarrow","description":"For those of you who remember Little Miss Wheelbarrow\u2014the hamster who was supposed to be \u201Cnormal,\u201D but came in with a very clearly not-normal leg \u{1F605}\u2014we have an exciting update!","date":"2025-06-19T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"},{"inline":false,"label":"Hamsters","permalink":"/blog/tags/hamsters","description":"Hamster updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.96,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jen Dupee","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/jen"},"imageURL":"/img/jen.jpg","key":"jen"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-19-wheelbarrow","title":"Wheelbarrow","authors":["jen"],"tags":["sanctuary","medical","hamsters"],"image":"./wb1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Adoption Alert - Snow White","permalink":"/blog/6-19-snowwhite"},"nextItem":{"title":"Team Wonkiest Boys","permalink":"/blog/6-18-update"}},"content":"For those of you who remember Little Miss Wheelbarrow\u2014the hamster who was supposed to be \u201Cnormal,\u201D but came in with a very clearly not-normal leg \u{1F605}\u2014we have an exciting update!\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nShe\u2019s now back in Northern New Jersey with Jen, and we\u2019re thrilled to have her there! A huge thank you to Danni\u2019s parents for taking such wonderful care of her and making sure she got to all her vet appointments.\\n\\nIt was determined that she had experienced a very significant break in her leg, but the good news is that it had mostly healed. After further x-rays and monitoring, the vet decided that because Anesthesia poses a high risk for small animals, and her leg was stable, amputation wasn\u2019t necessary.\\n\\nShe\u2019s doing fabulously\u2014her leg looks amazing, and she\u2019s getting around really well! She\u2019ll be staying on as a sanctuary hamster, though, since the injury increases her risk for early arthritis or other complications. We feel most comfortable keeping her with us for continued monitoring.\\n\\nThe best news? She\u2019s expected to live a normal, happy, healthy hamster life! \u{1F49B}\\n\\n![Wheelbarrow](wb1.jpg)\\n![Wheelbarrow](wb2.jpg)\\n![Wheelbarrow](wb3.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-18-update","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-18-update","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-18-update/2025-06-18-update.md","title":"Team Wonkiest Boys","description":"Hero is back to his popcorning self, and Parody is thriving\u2014he no longer needs to be hand-fed. He\u2019s eating, drinking, and his output is great. The only odd thing is that his stomach makes the wildest noises, which we suspect may be related to the mass in his abdomen.","date":"2025-06-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Lethal White","permalink":"/blog/tags/lethalwhite","description":"Lethal White guinea pigs"},{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.85,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-18-update","title":"Team Wonkiest Boys","authors":["danni"],"tags":["sanctuary","lethalwhite","medical","guineapigs"],"image":"./hero618.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Wheelbarrow","permalink":"/blog/6-19-wheelbarrow"},"nextItem":{"title":"Harold","permalink":"/blog/6-17-harold"}},"content":"Hero is back to his popcorning self, and Parody is thriving\u2014he no longer needs to be hand-fed. He\u2019s eating, drinking, and his output is great. The only odd thing is that his stomach makes the wildest noises, which we suspect may be related to the mass in his abdomen.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nWe also have a very interesting update about Novel. Since arriving, he hadn\u2019t been drinking on his own, so he\u2019s been getting sub-q fluids 1\u20133 times a day. A few days ago, while I was feeding him on a white blanket, he peed\u2014and it was quite pink! He was immediately started on antibiotics and is already feeling so much better. I even caught him nibbling on some pellets and hay!\\n\\nThis poor pig has been hiding so many health issues, but we\u2019re tackling them one by one\u2014and slowly seeing a much happier piggie emerge. It\u2019s been a slow process, but I\u2019m so glad I didn\u2019t give up on him\\n\\n![Hero](hero618.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-17-harold","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-17-harold","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-17-harold/2025-06-17-harold.md","title":"Harold","description":"We haven\u2019t posted about him in a while, but it\u2019s time for an update on everyone\u2019s favorite grumpy (and now confused) old man\u2014Harold.","date":"2025-06-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Hamsters","permalink":"/blog/tags/hamsters","description":"Hamster updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.68,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jen Dupee","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/jen"},"imageURL":"/img/jen.jpg","key":"jen"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-17-harold","title":"Harold","authors":["jen"],"tags":["sanctuary","hamsters"],"image":"./harold1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Team Wonkiest Boys","permalink":"/blog/6-18-update"},"nextItem":{"title":"Mistro","permalink":"/blog/6-17-mistro"}},"content":"We haven\u2019t posted about him in a while, but it\u2019s time for an update on everyone\u2019s favorite grumpy (and now confused) old man\u2014Harold.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nHarold is likely just over two years old now and is definitely showing his age. While we had hoped to find him a cozy retirement home, it looks like he\u2019ll be staying on as a hospice ham with me. (Which, of course, means he\u2019s probably going to live for years more \u{1F602}.)\\n\\nLately, I\u2019ve had to hold Harold\u2019s water bottle for him and gently squeeze it while he drinks. When it\u2019s attached to the cage, he just tries to pouch it. \u{1F605} If there\u2019s a hamster version of dementia, Harold most definitely has it. \u{1F974}\\n\\nBut we love him\u2014confused old man and all. \u{1F49B}\\n\\n![Harold](harold1.jpg)\\n![Harold](harold2.jpg)\\n![Harold](harold3.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-17-mistro","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-17-mistro","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-17-mistro/2025-06-17-mistro.md","title":"Mistro","description":"Certified Creeper","date":"2025-06-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.82,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jen Dupee","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/jen"},"imageURL":"/img/jen.jpg","key":"jen"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-17-mistro","title":"Mistro","authors":["jen"],"tags":["medical","sanctuary","guineapigs"],"image":"./mistro.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Harold","permalink":"/blog/6-17-harold"},"nextItem":{"title":"Adoption Alert - Sylvie, Checkers, Challah, and Fortune!","permalink":"/blog/6-8-adoptions"}},"content":"## Certified Creeper\\n\\nDid you know that in addition to guinea pigs, hamsters, rabbits (and pretty much everything else \u{1F602}), we also have a certified creeper living at Jen\u2019s house? Don\u2019t worry\u2014he\u2019s the cute kind. \u{1F609}\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nMistro was born right at Jen\u2019s house on April 21st, 2024\u2014yes, they share a birthday! He was always on the smaller side and had a few respiratory issues early on, so he and his brother ended up becoming permanent residents. A few months ago, Mistro developed a head tilt. We got him checked out and started treatment, but he decided he likes life with a tilt. \u{1F605} It\u2019s fine. Everything\u2019s fine. \u{1F974}\\n\\nThe best part? Mistro doesn\u2019t seem to notice at all\u2014he\u2019s happy, curious, and full of personality. He earned the nickname \u201Cthe creeper\u201D because he\u2019s always lurking from behind something, just watching with his adorable tilted head. \u{1F606}\\n\\n![Mistro](mistro.jpg)\\n![Mistro](mistro2.jpg)\\n\\nBut let\u2019s be honest: isn\u2019t he the most dashing little creeper you\u2019ve ever seen?\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-8-adoptions","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-8-adoptions","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-08-adoptions/2025-06-08-adoptions.md","title":"Adoption Alert - Sylvie, Checkers, Challah, and Fortune!","description":"\u{1F439}\u2728 Triple Adoption Celebration! \u2728\u{1F439}","date":"2025-06-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Adoptions","permalink":"/blog/tags/adoptions","description":"Adoption updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":1.43,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jen Dupee","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/jen"},"imageURL":"/img/jen.jpg","key":"jen"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-8-adoptions","title":"Adoption Alert - Sylvie, Checkers, Challah, and Fortune!","authors":["jen"],"tags":["adoptions","guineapigs"],"image":"./adoptions.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Mistro","permalink":"/blog/6-17-mistro"},"nextItem":{"title":"Saying Goodbye to Preston","permalink":"/blog/6-7-Preston"}},"content":"## \u{1F439}\u2728 Triple Adoption Celebration! \u2728\u{1F439}\\n\\nWe are so excited to share not one, not two, but three wonderful adoptions today!\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## \u{1F495} Sylvie & Checkers\\n\\nWe started the day with the exciting news that Sylvie and Checkers were adopted this morning! Their new family had previously adopted a guinea pig named Frazzle from us, but unfortunately, Frazzle wasn\u2019t getting along with one of their existing piggies.\\n\\nThey decided the best option was to keep Frazzle in her own space and find her a new friend\u2014but when they came to meet Sylvie and Checkers, they just couldn\u2019t choose\u2026 so they adopted both! Frazzle now has two new companions, and we couldn\u2019t be happier for all three!\\n\\n![Sylvie and Checkers](adoptions.jpg)\\n![Sylvie and Checkers](adoptions2.jpg)\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n## \u{1F956} Challah\\n\\nChallah also found her forever home today! She went home with one of our previous adopters, and as you can see, she\u2019s already fitting in beautifully with their group. She even matches the theme and looks like she\u2019s always belonged. We\u2019re so thrilled to see her thriving already!\\n\\n![Challah](adoptions3.jpg)\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n## \u{1F340} Fortune\\n\\nAnd to round out this amazing day\u2014Fortune has been adopted! This very handsome boy went home to a lovely family, including a senior guinea pig and an owner who happens to be a veterinarian! The plan is to have Fortune neutered and eventually start a small herd of females once their senior pig passes. We\u2019re absolutely overjoyed knowing he\u2019s in such knowledgeable and loving hands.\\n\\n![Fortune](adoptions4.jpg)\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\nIt\u2019s been a fantastic day for adoptions, and we are so grateful to our adopters, fosters, and supporters who make happy endings like these possible. \u{1F970} Stay tuned for more updates as we continue finding loving homes for these amazing little lives! \u{1F49B}\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-7-Preston","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-7-Preston","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-07-preston/2025-06-07-preston.md","title":"Saying Goodbye to Preston","description":"\u{1F494} Saying Goodbye to Preston \u{1F308}","date":"2025-06-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Rainbow Bridge","permalink":"/blog/tags/rainbowbridge","description":"Remembering the pets who have crossed the rainbow bridge."},{"inline":false,"label":"Chinchillas","permalink":"/blog/tags/chinchillas","description":"Chinchilla updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.82,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-7-Preston","title":"Saying Goodbye to Preston","authors":["danni"],"tags":["rainbowbridge","chinchillas"],"image":"./preston.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Adoption Alert - Sylvie, Checkers, Challah, and Fortune!","permalink":"/blog/6-8-adoptions"},"nextItem":{"title":"Baby Pigs are Growing Up!","permalink":"/blog/6-6-babies"}},"content":"## \u{1F494} Saying Goodbye to Preston \u{1F308}\\n\\nWe are absolutely heartbroken to share that we\u2019ll be bringing Preston in for humane euthanasia this afternoon.\\n\\nAround 3 AM, Preston suffered what appeared to be a neurological event. We were hoping it might be inner ear-related and started antibiotics right away, but sadly, there\u2019s been no improvement. We can\u2019t let him suffer, and this is the most compassionate option we have left.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n## \u{1F439} About Preston\\n\\nPreston came to us from the NHSPCA after being rescued from a hoarding situation. His previous owner had passed away, so we had very little background on his age or condition.\\n\\nHe was a sweet but shy boy who found comfort in his best friend, Sherlock. Letting him go is breaking our hearts.\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n## \u{1F4B8} Cost: $136\\n\\nThis loss was completely unexpected. If anyone is able to sponsor or co-sponsor Preston\u2019s euthanasia, it would mean the world to us. Even small contributions help in moments like this.\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n![Preston](preston.jpg)\\n![Preston](preston2.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-6-babies","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-6-babies","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-06-babies-growing-up/2025-06-06-babies-growing-up.md","title":"Baby Pigs are Growing Up!","description":"\u{1F393}  Not-So-Baby Girls Graduate!","date":"2025-06-06T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"News","permalink":"/blog/tags/news","description":"News and announcements"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":1.03,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-6-babies","title":"Baby Pigs are Growing Up!","authors":["danni"],"tags":["news","sanctuary","guineapigs"],"image":"./herd.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Saying Goodbye to Preston","permalink":"/blog/6-7-Preston"},"nextItem":{"title":"Haiku","permalink":"/blog/6-6-haiku"}},"content":"## \u{1F393}  Not-So-Baby Girls Graduate!\\n\\nOur \u201Cbabies\u201D aren\u2019t babies anymore\u2014Saga, Fantasy, Madrigal, Gothic, and Rhyme have officially graduated and joined the adult herd! These girls are now around 6\u20137 months old, and while we\u2019ll always think of them as our little ones, they\u2019re growing up beautifully.\\n\\nTheir move into the adult cage made space for a new special group\u2026\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n## \u{1F4AB} Introducing: Team Wonkiest Females\\n\\nWith their old cage freed up, we created a cozy setup for Team Wonkiest Females\u2014a crew of our beloved ladies who need a little extra care and monitoring:\\n\\nHaiku, Brynlee, Blakely, Memoir, and Coralie.\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n## \u{1F37D}\uFE0F Eating Update!\\n\\nThe good news keeps coming\u2014Coralie and Memoir are back to eating on their own, thanks to a solid pain management routine.\\n\\n - Novel is still our most hand-fed piggy right now, but he\u2019s making slow progress.\\n - Caper gets a little handfeeding too\u2026 but honestly? That\u2019s more because he\u2019s spoiled and likes the attention. \u{1F604}\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\nWe\u2019re celebrating the little wins every day\u2014whether it\u2019s a piggie eating on their own again, or a herd shuffle that just works. Thank you for supporting us as we care for these incredible little lives. \u{1F439}\u{1F495}\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n![Guinea Pig Herd](herd.jpg)\\n![Guinea Pig Herd - Team Wonkiest Females](herd2.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-6-haiku","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-6-haiku","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-06-haiku/2025-06-06-haiku.md","title":"Haiku","description":"\u{1F389} Meds Sponsored + Next Goal: Haiku\u2019s Bloodwork!","date":"2025-06-06T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"News","permalink":"/blog/tags/news","description":"News and announcements"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.76,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-6-haiku","title":"Haiku","authors":["danni"],"tags":["news","guineapigs"],"image":"./haiku1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Baby Pigs are Growing Up!","permalink":"/blog/6-6-babies"},"nextItem":{"title":"Adoptables - Danni, Casanova, Cupid","permalink":"/blog/6-5-adoptables"}},"content":"## \u{1F389} Meds Sponsored + Next Goal: Haiku\u2019s Bloodwork!\\n\\nGreat news\u2014Novel and Coralie\u2019s medications have been fully sponsored! \u{1F496} Thank you so much to everyone who helped make that happen!\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n\\n## \u{1F9EA} Next up on our urgent to-do list: Haiku\u2019s bloodwork\\n\\nHaiku has been struggling with some ongoing health concerns, and we\u2019re scheduled to do bloodwork at her next appointment:\\n\\n\u{1F4C5} Appointment Date: Tuesday, June 11th at 3 PM    \\n\u{1F4B0} Estimated Cost: ~$350    \\n(Includes the cost of light sedation, since she was very uncooperative at her last visit.)    \\n\\nIf she doesn\u2019t wind up needing sedation, any leftover funds will go directly toward another one of our special-needs critters.\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n## \u{1F439} Every donation is welcome!\\n\\nEven $5 or $10 makes a big difference when we all chip in. Let\u2019s get Haiku the answers she needs\u2014and hopefully a solid plan for moving forward with treatment.\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n![Haiku](haiku1.jpg)\\n![Haiku](haiku2.jpg)\\n![Haiku](haiku3.jpg)\\n![Haiku](haiku4.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-5-adoptables","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-5-adoptables","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-05-danni-casanova-cupid/2025-06-05-danni-casanova-cupid.md","title":"Adoptables - Danni, Casanova, Cupid","description":"\u{1F439} Danni, Casanova & Cupid Check In! \u{1F48C}","date":"2025-06-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Adoptables","permalink":"/blog/tags/adoptables","description":"Animals available for adoption"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":1.08,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jen Dupee","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/jen"},"imageURL":"/img/jen.jpg","key":"jen"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-5-adoptables","title":"Adoptables - Danni, Casanova, Cupid","authors":["jen"],"tags":["adoptables","guineapigs"],"image":"./adoptables.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Haiku","permalink":"/blog/6-6-haiku"},"nextItem":{"title":"Vet Day Recap - Sherlock and Coralie","permalink":"/blog/6-4-nh-update"}},"content":"## \u{1F439} Danni, Casanova & Cupid Check In! \u{1F48C}\\n\\nDanni (the pig, not the person), Casanova, and Cupid are checking in from their wonderful foster home\u2014and these handsome boys are doing great!\\n\\nCasanova and Cupid met by chance and headed off to foster together. While Casanova was a bit of a drama starter at first (aren\u2019t all the best leading men?), things have really smoothed out. There are still the occasional kerfuffles when everyone gets the zoomies and crashes into each other, but now that they\u2019ve settled in, their personalities are really starting to shine.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThese boys came to us from the Woodbridge guinea pig group and were pretty beat up when they arrived.\\n\\n\u{1F4AB} Danni has been with us since he was just 3 weeks old\u2014his birthday is April 21st!\u2014and he\u2019s been patiently waiting for his forever home ever since.\\n\\nNow that they\u2019ve bonded into a sweet trio (especially Danni and Cupid, who are clearly besties), we\u2019re looking to adopt them out together. And let\u2019s be honest\u2026\\n\\nThree guinea pigs are better than one. \u{1F609}\u{1F495}\\n\\nIf you\u2019re ready to add some charm, cuddles, and zoomie chaos to your life, these boys are ready to go home!\\n\\n\u{1F4E9} Apply to adopt via our Petfinder or website today!\\n\\n![CGuinea Pig](adoptables.jpg)\\n\\n![CGuinea Pig](adoptables2.jpg)\\n\\n![CGuinea Pig](adoptables3.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-4-nh-update","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-4-nh-update","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-04-nj-update/2025-06-04-nj-update.md","title":"Vet Day Recap - Sherlock and Coralie","description":"\u{1F43E} Vet Day Recap + Chaos Incoming","date":"2025-06-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"News","permalink":"/blog/tags/news","description":"News and announcements"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Chinchillas","permalink":"/blog/tags/chinchillas","description":"Chinchilla updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Satin Syndrome","permalink":"/blog/tags/satin","description":"Satin syndrome in guinea pigs"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Medical","permalink":"/blog/tags/medical","description":"Medical updates and health topics"}],"readingTime":2.55,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-4-nh-update","title":"Vet Day Recap - Sherlock and Coralie","authors":["danni"],"tags":["news","guineapigs","chinchillas","satin","sanctuary","medical"],"image":"./vet6-4.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Adoptables - Danni, Casanova, Cupid","permalink":"/blog/6-5-adoptables"},"nextItem":{"title":"Update on Sherlock and Coralie","permalink":"/blog/6-4-sherlock-coralie"}},"content":"## \u{1F43E} Vet Day Recap + Chaos Incoming\\n\\nCurrently sitting in my car, kind of dreading walking into my house. Between the disaster zone that is my living room and the stack of things I need to do\u2014on top of handfeeding multiple piggies\u2014I\u2019m feeling more than a little twitchy. But anyway\u2026 here\u2019s today\u2019s vet report!\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n\\n## \u{1F9B7} Sherlock\\n\\nSherlock was an absolute champ. He even ate Oxbow pellets after waking up from surgery\u2014this blew my mind, because at home, if I offer him Oxbow, he dumps the bowl and pees in it out of spite.\\n\\nHe has a recheck in a month to see how soon we\u2019ll need to schedule the next dental. Oh, and fun fact? He has a bonus rogue tooth growing in a totally inappropriate location. Honestly, that tracks. Of course the animal living in my house has an extra, chaotic tooth.\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n## \u{1F439} A Fun Surprise!\\n\\nI finally got to meet Betsy from Wheektopia Rescue in person today at Dr. Ford\u2019s office! I recommended Southern Maine to her a while back, and she loved them so much she made them her primary vet too.\\n\\nIt was so nice to meet another fellow crazy guinea pig person in real life\u2014and yes, I got to smooch some of the adorable pigs she brought in for exams. Check out this massive mama-to-be! I had to head out before her ultrasound, but I\u2019ll be texting Betsy later to see how many babies are packed in there.\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n## \u{1F614} Poor Coralie\\n\\nIn not-so-great news: Coralie has very advanced osteodystrophy in her jaw, and it\u2019s incredibly worn down. Honestly, I don\u2019t know how she hid it for so long\u2014girl can EAT.\\n\\nShe also has significant bone wear around her knees. While I often use \u201CSatin Syndrome\u201D as a general term, osteodystrophy specifically refers to when the body stops absorbing calcium properly, causing the bones to erode or break down. Satin Syndrome is more of a catch-all term for the various issues that satins can face.\\n\\nLooking into her mouth, you\u2019ll see red sores\u2014not because of bad teeth (hers are actually great), but likely from chewing awkwardly to avoid jaw pain.\\n\\nCoralie and Novel will now be on stronger pain meds 2x/day, compared to the other satins, who are doing well on once-daily higher-dose Metacam.\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n## \u{1F3D7}\uFE0F Housing Shuffle Incoming\\n\\nI\u2019m tinkering with the idea of grouping the satin girls together in one cage to reduce food competition and give them a more consistent care setup.\\n\\nI\u2019m also considering moving Haiku, Kavita, Brynlee, and Blakely into their own setup since Haiku and Brynlee have kidney issues, and I\u2019d like to try them on 100% Timothy pellets.\\n\\nSo yes\u2026 it\u2019s going to be a very long weekend of playing musical guinea pigs. \u{1F643}\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\nThanks as always for following our chaos and supporting our crew. If you\u2019d like to help us through this endless wave of medical needs and cage reconfigurations, please check out our donation info! \u{1F495}\u{1F439}\\n\\n\\n![Vet Invoice](vet6-4.jpg)\\n\\n![GDental](dental.jpg)\\n\\n![Chunky Momma Pig](chunkymomma.jpg)\\n\\n![X Ray](xray.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-4-sherlock-coralie","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-4-sherlock-coralie","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-04-sherlock-coralie/2025-06-04-sherlock-coralie.md","title":"Update on Sherlock and Coralie","description":"\u{1F9B7} Sherlock Update + Urgent Help Needed!","date":"2025-06-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"News","permalink":"/blog/tags/news","description":"News and announcements"},{"inline":false,"label":"Satin Syndrome","permalink":"/blog/tags/satin","description":"Satin syndrome in guinea pigs"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Chinchillas","permalink":"/blog/tags/chinchillas","description":"Chinchilla updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.99,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-4-sherlock-coralie","title":"Update on Sherlock and Coralie","authors":["danni"],"tags":["news","satin","sanctuary","chinchillas","guineapigs"],"image":"./sherlock.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Vet Day Recap - Sherlock and Coralie","permalink":"/blog/6-4-nh-update"},"nextItem":{"title":"Update on Haiku","permalink":"/blog/haiku"}},"content":"## \u{1F9B7} Sherlock Update + Urgent Help Needed!\\n\\nWe really need to take some updated glamour shots of Sherlock, because this brave boy made it through dental surgery like a champ! \u{1F4AA}\\n\\nAs expected, his teeth were a complete mess\u2014including a random spare tooth just hanging out where it didn\u2019t belong. We\u2019re likely looking at dental surgeries every 6 weeks to keep him healthy and comfortable.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n### \u{1F439} Sherlock\u2019s Favorites (for anyone who wants to spoil him!)\\n\\n - \u{1F963} Pellets: Science Selective\\n - \u{1F372} Recovery Food: Sherwood Recovery (he eats bowlfuls!)\\n - \u{1F353} Treats: A tiny dried Oxbow strawberry after meds = pure joy\\n\\n### \u{1F4B8} Estimated Costs\\n\\n - \u{1F9B7} Sherlock\u2019s dental surgery: ~$400\\n(We\u2019re waiting for the final invoice since he\u2019s still in recovery)\\n\\nWe would love to find some sponsors to help cover his ongoing care. If you\u2019ve ever considered sponsoring a special-needs animal\u2014this is your guy. \u{1F43E}\\n\\n![Sherlock the Chinchilla](sherlock.jpg)\\n\\n## \u{1F6A8} New Concern: Coralie\\n\\nLast night during veggie time, Coralie (one of our beautiful satin girls) refused her peppers\u2014her absolute favorite. That\u2019s a big red flag.\\n\\nShe\u2019s heading to the vet at 3 PM today, and we\u2019re anticipating about $150 for her exam and X-rays to check for Satin Syndrome.\\n\\n![Coralie the Guinea Pig](coralie.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"haiku","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/haiku","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-03-haiku/2025-06-03-haiku.md","title":"Update on Haiku","description":"\u{1FA7A} Medical Update + Urgent Funding Need","date":"2025-06-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"News","permalink":"/blog/tags/news","description":"News and announcements"},{"inline":false,"label":"Educational","permalink":"/blog/tags/educational","description":"Educational content"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":1.43,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"haiku","title":"Update on Haiku","authors":["danni"],"tags":["news","educational","sanctuary","guineapigs"],"image":"./haiku.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Update on Sherlock and Coralie","permalink":"/blog/6-4-sherlock-coralie"},"nextItem":{"title":"Update on Parody and Novel","permalink":"/blog/6-3-parody-novel"}},"content":"## \u{1FA7A} Medical Update + Urgent Funding Need\\n\\n### Haiku\\n\\nHaiku\u2019s urinalysis results just came back and showed:\\n\\n - Very low specific gravity, and\\n - Crystals in her urine\\n\\nThis is a first for us\u2014we\u2019ve never had a piggy with crystals before\u2014but in theory, it should be manageable. Everyone here is already on a fairly low-calcium diet, but we\u2019ll be separating Haiku from the herd temporarily and adjusting her diet to an even more kidney-friendly plan.\\n\\nGiven how underweight she was at intake, we suspect this issue started long before she arrived at the rescue.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n### \u{1F9EA} What\u2019s Next?\\n\\nWe\u2019d like to do bloodwork to rule out or confirm kidney disease or hyperthyroidism\u2014both of which could explain her weight loss despite a healthy appetite.\\n\\nHyperthyroidism can impact kidney function, but it\u2019s also very manageable with a proper diagnosis. We need confirmation to move forward with the right treatment plan.\\n\\nThere\u2019s a chance we may need to lightly sedate her for the blood draw since she was extremely uncooperative at her last exam. We\u2019ll be at the vet tomorrow for Sherlock\u2019s dental, and we\u2019re hoping to get her bloodwork done then if we can raise the funds in time.\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n\u{1F4B8} Here\u2019s What We Need Help Covering\\n\\n - \u{1F431} Rabies boosters for sanctuary cats (due to bat exposure)\\t$250\\n - \u{1F439} Novel & Parody\u2019s vet visit $208\\n - \u{1F439} Sherlock, Haiku, Kavita, Caper, Rhyme & Memoir\u2019s visit (6/5)\\t$358\\n - \u{1F9B7} Sherlock\u2019s dental surgery (6/6)\\t$350\\n - \u{1F9EA} Haiku\u2019s bloodwork (incl. potential sedation)\\t$350\\n\\n\u{1F198} Total Needed: **$1,516**\\n\\nIf we don\u2019t end up needing the full amount for Haiku\u2019s bloodwork, the remaining funds will roll over to cover other vet bills, as we\u2019re absolutely drowning in medical costs right now. Every dollar helps. Every share helps.\\n\\n![Haiku the Guinea Pig](haiku.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-3-parody-novel","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-3-parody-novel","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-03-parody-novel/2025-06-03-parody-novel.md","title":"Update on Parody and Novel","description":"\u{1F48A} Pain Management Update + A Sweet Social Shift","date":"2025-06-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"News","permalink":"/blog/tags/news","description":"News and announcements"},{"inline":false,"label":"Educational","permalink":"/blog/tags/educational","description":"Educational content"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":1.08,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-3-parody-novel","title":"Update on Parody and Novel","authors":["danni"],"tags":["news","educational","sanctuary","guineapigs"],"image":"./parody.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Update on Haiku","permalink":"/blog/haiku"},"nextItem":{"title":"From the Desk of Susan from Marketing and Carol from HR","permalink":"/blog/biz-pigs"}},"content":"## \u{1F48A} Pain Management Update + A Sweet Social Shift\\n\\nBoth Parody and Novel had a really positive response to the addition of Buprenex to their pain management plans!\\n\\nLast night, Novel was eating celery on his own, and this afternoon, Parody munched on some pellets and veggies without help. It\u2019s clear the medication helped\u2014but just as clear when it wears off. Since Buprenex is a controlled substance, vets have to be careful with how they prescribe it. We were given just one dose each to test their response.\\n\\nNow that we know it helps, we should be able to get a bit more at a time\u2014but I expect I\u2019ll still need to make regular trips to the vet to pick it up. (Not a problem\u2014I\u2019m there at least once a week anyway!)\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## \u{1F439} A New Roommate and a Little Surprise\\n\\nI also took a chance and moved Parody into the couch cage, and so far he\u2019s doing great! He\u2019s very calm and seems completely smitten with Hero. Honestly, the babies really seem to lift everyone\u2019s spirits\u2014mine included.\\n\\nAnd even as a lifelong crazy guinea pig lady, I never thought I\u2019d be the kind of person with a couch cage\u2026\\nBut here we are. \u{1F481}\u200D\u2640\uFE0F\u{1F439}\u{1F495}\\n\\n![Parody the Guinea Pig](parody.jpg)\\n\\n![Novel the Guinea Pig](novel.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"biz-pigs","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/biz-pigs","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-02-business-pigs/2025-06-02-business-pigs.md","title":"From the Desk of Susan from Marketing and Carol from HR","description":"From the Desk of Susan from Marketing and Carol from HR","date":"2025-06-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Adoptables","permalink":"/blog/tags/adoptables","description":"Animals available for adoption"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":1.47,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jen Dupee","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/jen"},"imageURL":"/img/jen.jpg","key":"jen"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"biz-pigs","title":"From the Desk of Susan from Marketing and Carol from HR","authors":["jen"],"tags":["adoptables","guineapigs"],"image":"./bizpig1.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Update on Parody and Novel","permalink":"/blog/6-3-parody-novel"},"nextItem":{"title":"Vet Updates from the NH Branch","permalink":"/blog/6-2-25-nh-update"}},"content":"## From the Desk of Susan from Marketing and Carol from HR\\n\\n\u{1F4E2} Please have a seat\u2014this won\u2019t take long, but it is a mandatory meeting.\\n\\nSusan from Marketing and Carol from HR have prepared a brief presentation on why you need business pigs in your life\u2014and how they can help you solve all your productivity problems!\\n\\nThere will be a PowerPoint presentation (because would it even be a real business meeting without one?).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nSusan from Marketing will be leading the bulk of today\u2019s session. She and Carol firmly believe that every home needs business guinea pigs. What do they bring to the table?\\n - First, they\u2019re excellent at keeping everyone on schedule. They know when it\u2019s veggie time and will politely remind you\u2014by screaming into the void\u2014if you\u2019re even a minute late.\\n - They\u2019re highly motivating. Who wouldn\u2019t want to work harder for these tiny, adorable faces?\\n - They bring structured fun to the workplace\u2014think of it as business-appropriate enrichment. Maybe a veggie party at lunch to boost team morale?\\n\\nNow, let\u2019s address logistics. Susan and Carol are a bonded pair and must stay together. They\u2019re a dynamic duo, and let\u2019s be honest: two business pigs are always better than one.\\n\\nWhile they believe everyone could benefit from business pigs in their lives, they are the only ones currently looking to transfer offices. So don\u2019t miss your chance\u2014submit your hiring packets soon!\\n\\n\u{1F4E9} That concludes today\u2019s presentation. Susan and Carol are happy to take questions, but if you\u2019re already sold, applications can be found on our Petfinder!\\n\\nBonus: They\u2019d be thrilled to join an existing team of business pigs, if you\u2019ve already got an established office looking for fresh talent.\\n\\n![susan and carol](bizpig1.jpg)\\n\\n![susan and carol](bizpig2.jpg)"},{"id":"6-2-25-nh-update","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-2-25-nh-update","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-02-nh-update/2025-06-02-nh-update.md","title":"Vet Updates from the NH Branch","description":"Today was a very busy day at the vet. Thankfully, nothing was outright terrible\u2014but we did learn some surprising things about a few of our little ones. Here\u2019s a full update!","date":"2025-06-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"News","permalink":"/blog/tags/news","description":"News and announcements"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Chinchillas","permalink":"/blog/tags/chinchillas","description":"Chinchilla updates and stories"},{"inline":false,"label":"Lethal White","permalink":"/blog/tags/lethalwhite","description":"Lethal White guinea pigs"}],"readingTime":2.58,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-2-25-nh-update","title":"Vet Updates from the NH Branch","authors":["danni"],"tags":["news","guineapigs","sanctuary","chinchillas","lethalwhite"],"image":"./nh3.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"From the Desk of Susan from Marketing and Carol from HR","permalink":"/blog/biz-pigs"},"nextItem":{"title":"Updates from the NH Branch","permalink":"/blog/6-1-25-nh-update"}},"content":"Today was a very busy day at the vet. Thankfully, nothing was outright terrible\u2014but we did learn some surprising things about a few of our little ones. Here\u2019s a full update!\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n### \u{1F42D} Sherlock the Chinchilla\\n\\nNo surprise here\u2014Sherlock needs a dental surgery, which is scheduled for Wednesday. We absolutely need help covering the cost of this procedure. Donations, shares, and good vibes are all deeply appreciated.\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n### \u{1F439} Caper\\n\\nCaper came in for a recheck. I just wanted to be absolutely sure that his drooling was from having no incisors (rather than bad molars). His molars look fantastic!\\n\\nCaper is actually the first small animal I\u2019ve had with zero incisors\u2014I\u2019ve seen many missing one or two, but not all four. He\u2019s growing slowly and is much smaller than his brother, and may just be a tiny guy for life.\\n\\nSadly, based on past experience, very stunted pigs often have shorter life spans due to poor genetics. I knew this going in, but it doesn\u2019t make it any easier. These babies have my whole heart, and when their time comes, I will not be okay.\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n### \u{1F439} Kavita\\n\\nKavita got her second hormone injection today, and it\u2019s already helping! Her hair is growing back and the ovarian cysts have shrunk significantly after just one dose. We\u2019re hopeful this second round will resolve things completely.\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n### \u{1F439} Haiku\\n\\nHaiku was meant to be seen just for a heart check, but she was very painful and grumpy with the tech and Dr. Ford\u2014very unlike her.\\n\\nShe\u2019s been eating well and has great poops, but has lost weight. We were discussing possible arthritis and other ideas when she urinated on the table\u2014and all three of us noticed how diluted her urine looked.\\n\\nWe took a sample and sent it for testing. Kidney issues would explain the weight loss\u2026 and while her heart sounded good today, possible kidney disease is deeply concerning.\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n### \u{1F439} Rhyme (Mother of the Lethals)\\n\\nI brought Rhyme in to confirm that she wasn\u2019t pregnant again (she\u2019s not). During the ultrasound, however, we discovered that her colon has adhered to her bladder\u2014a not-uncommon result of pregnancy and birth.\\n\\nThere\u2019s nothing to be done now, but it\u2019s critical information in case she ever needs abdominal surgery.\\n\\nWe also saw that her bladder looked inflamed, even though she has no UTI symptoms. She\u2019s starting a round of antibiotics and we\u2019ll recheck her soon.\\n\\n\u2E3B\\n\\n### \u{1F439} Memoir\\n\\nMemoir is a stunning satin girl who arrived just under a month ago. At intake, she was bunny hopping, which usually indicates arthritis or osteodystrophy.\\n\\nHer X-rays didn\u2019t show any bone loss at that time, so we started her on a low-dose pain med. Unfortunately, her pain is already increasing\u2014a sign that Satin Syndrome may be progressing.\\n\\nWe\u2019re increasing her dose of Metacam and hoping it will bring her some much-needed comfort. Satin Syndrome is heartbreaking and can advance so quickly.\\n\\n----\\n\\n![Guinea Pig](nh3.jpg)\\n\\n![Guinea Pig](nh4.jpg)\\n\\n![Guinea Pig](nh5.jpg)\\n\\n![Guinea Pig](nh6.jpg)\\n\\n![Guinea Pig](nh7.jpg)\\n\\n![Guinea Pig](nh8.jpg)\\n\\n![Vet Bill](vet6-2.jpg)\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"6-1-25-nh-update","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/6-1-25-nh-update","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-01-nh-update/2025-06-01-nh-update.md","title":"Updates from the NH Branch","description":"We have some sad news, but also some cautiously hopeful updates to share.","date":"2025-06-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"News","permalink":"/blog/tags/news","description":"News and announcements"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":1.67,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Danielle Challender","title":"President, New Hampshire Sanctuary","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/danni"},"imageURL":"/img/danni.jpg","key":"danni"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"6-1-25-nh-update","title":"Updates from the NH Branch","authors":["danni"],"tags":["news","guineapigs"],"image":"./kerrigan.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Vet Updates from the NH Branch","permalink":"/blog/6-2-25-nh-update"},"nextItem":{"title":"Adoption Alert - Po","permalink":"/blog/po"}},"content":"We have some sad news, but also some cautiously hopeful updates to share.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n\u{1F494} First, the heartbreaking part:\\n\\n### Kerrigan\\n\\nOur sweet Kerrigan, who had been living with congestive heart failure, passed away peacefully this morning. Kerrigan was born in the rescue on May 27, 2020, and was adopted as a baby before returning to us last June. I had a feeling she belonged with us, so she joined our large female herd, where she was surrounded by friends and love.\\n\\nNot long after her return, she was diagnosed with CHF. With Lasix and Pimobendan, we were able to manage her condition for quite a while. Kerrigan was a happy, affectionate girl who adored her herd and the extra cuddles she got during medication time. She\u2019s now reunited with her sister\u2014hopefully popcorning together over the rainbow bridge. \u{1F495}\\n\\n### Novel and Parody\\n\\n\u{1F331} Now for some cautiously good news:\\nThe round-the-clock care over the past few weeks has been exhausting\u2014but it\u2019s starting to feel worth it. Our lethal babies are thriving, and both Novel and Parody seem to have turned a corner.\\n\\n\u2728 Novel is now holding himself upright instead of lying flat and floppy. Truthfully, I\u2019ve never had success bringing back an animal once they\u2019ve reached that point\u2014but Novel is alert, interested in food, has started urinating, and even let out a little protest during his last sub-q fluids. These are all wonderful signs.\\n\\n\u2728 Parody is full of curiosity and taking food like a champ. His poops are well-formed\u2014still not quite as much as we\u2019d like, but any output right now is a win.\\n\\nAfter days of being glued to their sides, terrified to step away, I finally feel like I can breathe a bit. I may even take a shower and grab some fresh veggies for the crew. (I also need to get my second rabies shot after that bat incident, but that\u2019s another story\u2026)\\n\\n![Kerrigan the Guinea Pig](kerrigan.jpg)\\n\\n![Guinea Pig](nh.jpg)\\n\\n![Guinea Pig](nh2.jpg)\\n\\n----\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"po","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/po","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-01-po/2025-06-01-po.md","title":"Adoption Alert - Po","description":"We\u2019re thrilled to share that Po found his forever home this weekend\u2014with one of our longtime friends and supporters! He\u2019s already on his way to living a very spoiled life, and we couldn\u2019t be more excited that he\u2019s with someone we know and trust.","date":"2025-06-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Adoptions","permalink":"/blog/tags/adoptions","description":"Adoption updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Hamsters","permalink":"/blog/tags/hamsters","description":"Hamster updates and stories"}],"readingTime":0.38,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jen Dupee","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/jen"},"imageURL":"/img/jen.jpg","key":"jen"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"po","title":"Adoption Alert - Po","authors":["jen"],"tags":["adoptions","hamsters"],"image":"./po.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Updates from the NH Branch","permalink":"/blog/6-1-25-nh-update"},"nextItem":{"title":"Meet the Board of Directors","permalink":"/blog/board"}},"content":"We\u2019re thrilled to share that Po found his forever home this weekend\u2014with one of our longtime friends and supporters! He\u2019s already on his way to living a very spoiled life, and we couldn\u2019t be more excited that he\u2019s with someone we know and trust.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nEven better? We\u2019re promised regular updates, so we\u2019ll get to see all of Po\u2019s adventures as he settles into his new kingdom. \u{1F49B}\\n\\nHappy tails, Po! \u{1F439}\u2728\\n\\n![po the hamster](po.jpg)"},{"id":"board","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/board","source":"@site/blog/2024-10-23-meet-the-board/2024-10-23-meet-the-board.mdx","title":"Meet the Board of Directors","description":"We want to be open and transparent with our followers and supporters about who\'s running things, so we\'re thrilled to introduce our Board of Directors!","date":"2024-10-23T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Humans","permalink":"/blog/tags/humans","description":"Posts about our volunteers and team"}],"readingTime":3.43,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Alanna Burke","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/alanna"},"imageURL":"/img/alanna.jpg","key":"alanna"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"board","title":"Meet the Board of Directors","authors":["alanna"],"tags":["humans"],"image":"./danni.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Adoption Alert - Po","permalink":"/blog/po"},"nextItem":{"title":"Fundraiser for Boba","permalink":"/blog/boba"}},"content":"import styles from \'./board.module.css\';\\n\\nWe want to be open and transparent with our followers and supporters about who\'s running things, so we\'re thrilled to introduce our Board of Directors!\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n<div className={styles.boardGrid}>\\n\\n<div className={styles.boardCard}>\\n  <div className={styles.boardPhotoWrap}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./danni.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Danni Challender, President of HALT\\" className={styles.boardPhoto} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div className={styles.boardInfo}>\\n    <span className={styles.boardRole}>President</span>\\n    <h3 className={styles.boardName}>Danni Challender</h3>\\n    <p>Danni started Helping All Little Things in August of 2010 when her best friend\'s rescue was overwhelmed with guinea pig surrenders.</p>\\n    <p><em>Fun fact: Danni was terrified of guinea pigs when she was a child!</em> Danni currently runs the sanctuary from her home in New Hampshire. She houses senior, hospice, and special needs small animals and gives them all the care they need to live a normal, happy life. She also manages our foster team which are based in NJ, PA, NY and DE.</p>\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div className={styles.boardCard}>\\n  <div className={styles.boardPhotoWrap}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./ericka.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Ericka Chadbourne, Vice President of HALT\\" className={styles.boardPhoto} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div className={styles.boardInfo}>\\n    <span className={styles.boardRole}>Vice President</span>\\n    <h3 className={styles.boardName}>Ericka Chadbourne</h3>\\n    <p>Ericka has been involved in animal rescue since 2000 when two little feral kittens entered her life unexpectedly. Their intro initiated 24 years of devoted loving work in rescue. Ericka started rescuing small animals about 14 years ago when she became heavily involved in HALT.</p>\\n    <p>She now provides sanctuary to two hedgehogs and six chinchillas while supporting the rescue in other ways. Ericka heads up the arts and crafts division while collecting items for and running auctions. Her primary focus now is to collaborate with others on raising funds that help cover medical care and provide rich and fulfilling lives for the animals in the rescue\'s care. Ericka shares her house with the chinchillas, hedgies and with four lovely kitties who she is devoted to entirely.</p>\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div className={styles.boardCard}>\\n  <div className={styles.boardPhotoWrap}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./alanna.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Alanna Burke, Secretary of HALT\\" className={styles.boardPhoto} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div className={styles.boardInfo}>\\n    <span className={styles.boardRole}>Secretary</span>\\n    <h3 className={styles.boardName}>Alanna Burke</h3>\\n    <p>Alanna has always been involved in rescue, mostly working with cats, but became a part of HALT in 2017 when she adopted rats and then guinea pigs. Alanna handles all things technical and web-related for the rescue, as well as most of the graphics. She currently has 7 guinea pigs, 3 cats, and a dog.</p>\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div className={styles.boardCard}>\\n  <div className={styles.boardPhotoWrap}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./lynne.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Lynne Blecher, Treasurer of HALT\\" className={styles.boardPhoto} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div className={styles.boardInfo}>\\n    <span className={styles.boardRole}>Treasurer</span>\\n    <h3 className={styles.boardName}>Lynne Blecher</h3>\\n    <p>Lynne is a passionate animal advocate, dedicating her life to giving pets and humans a second chance at a good life. Lynne has both fostered and adopted unwanted pets since she was a teenager \u2014 from reptiles, amphibians, birds, guinea pigs, mice, rats, fish, dogs and cats.</p>\\n    <p>Lynne is currently a member of Bucks County Aquarium Society who teaches the public how to ethically keep fish and supports the CARES program which works to keep endangered fish species alive. Her family includes her husband, two rescue dogs, an African Grey parrot, a 90 gallon aquarium and one Axolotl.</p>\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div className={styles.boardCard}>\\n  <div className={styles.boardPhotoWrap}>\\n    <img src={require(\'./jen.jpg\').default} alt=\\"Jen Dupee, Board Member of HALT\\" className={styles.boardPhoto} />\\n  </div>\\n  <div className={styles.boardInfo}>\\n    <span className={styles.boardRole}>Board Member</span>\\n    <h3 className={styles.boardName}>Jen Dupee</h3>\\n    <p>Jen has been involved in animal rescue for a number of years. She started working with wildlife rehabilitation at the age of 16, and evolved into domestic animal rescue in 2017. Jen became involved with HALT about 5 years ago.</p>\\n    <p>Her primary focus is New Jersey-based surrenders as well as New Jersey-based sanctuary cases. She currently has a variety of different animals she shares a house with, including cats, dogs, rabbits, guinea pigs and a variety of other smaller exotics, insects, and arachnids.</p>\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"boba","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/boba","source":"@site/blog/2024-10-09-boba/2024-10-09-boba.md","title":"Fundraiser for Boba","description":"Boba Update!","date":"2024-10-09T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Fundraiser","permalink":"/blog/tags/fundraiser","description":"Fundraisers and campaigns"},{"inline":false,"label":"Sanctuary","permalink":"/blog/tags/sanctuary","description":"Sanctuary life and updates"},{"inline":false,"label":"Guinea Pigs","permalink":"/blog/tags/guineapigs","description":"Guinea pig updates and stories"}],"readingTime":3.51,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Alanna Burke","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/alanna"},"imageURL":"/img/alanna.jpg","key":"alanna"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"boba","title":"Fundraiser for Boba","authors":["alanna"],"tags":["fundraiser","sanctuary","guineapigs"],"image":"./boba-incision.jpeg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Meet the Board of Directors","permalink":"/blog/board"},"nextItem":{"title":"New T Shirt Fundraiser!","permalink":"/blog/tshirt2024"}},"content":"## Boba Update!\\n\\nOn October 10, Boba had his surgery at Southern Maine Hospital for Small Mammals, and it was a big success! The tumor was removed with excellent margins, and Boba came through surgery perfectly, eating and pooping as soon as he woke up. The incision is so small and clean, and we\'re so grateful to Dr Ford for his excellent work! Boba will be recovering with some pain meds for a few days, but we expect him to be back to normal ASAP.\\n\\n![Boba\'s Incision](boba-incision.jpeg)\\n\\n# Fundraiser for Boba: Help Our Brave Guinea Pig Fight Lymphoma\\n\\nMeet Boba, a remarkable 7-year-old guinea pig with a heart full of resilience and the spirit of a fighter. Boba has faced more than his fair share of challenges in his life, but he never gave up\u2014and now, he needs our help to overcome his toughest battle yet. Boba has been diagnosed with lymphoma, and he\u2019s scheduled to undergo surgery to remove a cancerous tumor. We are raising funds to cover the cost of his surgery and post-operative care, and every donation\u2014no matter how small\u2014will make a huge difference.\\n\\n[You can read more about lymphoma in guinea pigs here.](/docs/guinea-pigs/illnesses-and-conditions/lymphoma)\\n\\n![Boba](boba2.jpeg)\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Boba\u2019s Journey: A True Survivor\\n\\nBoba\u2019s story is one of courage and survival. He\u2019s not just any guinea pig\u2014he\u2019s a warrior who has been through many health challenges over the years. At 7 years old, Boba has already lived a full life, but it hasn\u2019t been without its struggles:\\n\\n\\\\- **Missing an Eyeball**: Boba lost one of his eyes earlier in life, but that never slowed him down. He learned to navigate the world with one eye, always full of curiosity and determination.\\n\\n\\\\- **Surviving Bumblefoot**: Boba also battled and beat bumblefoot, a painful condition affecting the feet of guinea pigs. With proper treatment, care, and a lot of love, Boba healed and bounced back stronger than ever.\\n\\nNow, Boba is facing another hurdle: lymphoma. Despite his age and past health issues, Boba\u2019s spirit remains strong, and we believe he deserves every chance to live out his golden years cancer-free.\\n\\n## Why Boba Needs Your Help\\n\\n![Boba\'s tumor](boba.jpeg)\\n\\n\\nBoba\u2019s upcoming surgery is essential to removing the cancerous tumor, but it comes with significant costs, including:\\n\\n\\\\- **Surgical Expenses**: The procedure to remove Boba\u2019s tumor is complex and requires expert veterinary care.\\n\\n\\\\- **Post-Operative Care**: After surgery, Boba will need specialized care to ensure a smooth recovery. This includes medications, follow-up vet visits, and supportive care at home.\\n\\nAs a small, volunteer-run animal rescue, we rely on the generosity of our supporters to give guinea pigs like Boba the medical care they need. Every dollar raised will go directly towards covering Boba\u2019s surgery and recovery expenses.\\n\\n## How You Can Help Boba\\n\\nWe are asking for your help to give Boba a fighting chance. Here\u2019s how you can support his surgery and recovery:\\n\\n1\\\\. **Donate**: Every donation, no matter the amount, brings us one step closer to funding Boba\u2019s surgery. Whether it\u2019s $5 or $50, your contribution will make a difference.\\n\\n2\\\\. **Share**: If you\u2019re unable to donate, you can still help by sharing Boba\u2019s story with your friends, family, and social media followers. The more people who know about Boba\u2019s journey, the closer we\u2019ll get to reaching our goal.\\n\\n3\\\\. **Send Love**: Boba may not understand words, but he knows love and kindness. Leave a comment or message of support for Boba to let him know how many people are rooting for him\\\\!\\n\\n## Boba\u2019s Future Depends on You\\n\\nBoba has shown time and again that he\u2019s a survivor, and we believe he can overcome lymphoma with the right medical care. But we can\u2019t do it without your help. Let\u2019s rally together to support this brave little guy and give him the chance to live out his remaining years in comfort and good health.\\n\\nThank you so much for your generosity and compassion. Boba may be missing an eye, but he\u2019s never lost his sight for the love and support around him. Together, we can help Boba fight this cancer and continue to live his best life.\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"tshirt2024","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/tshirt2024","source":"@site/blog/2024-09-09-tshirt/2024-09-09-tshirt.md","title":"New T Shirt Fundraiser!","description":"Introducing Our New Rescue T-Shirts!","date":"2024-09-09T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Fundraiser","permalink":"/blog/tags/fundraiser","description":"Fundraisers and campaigns"}],"readingTime":1.01,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Alanna Burke","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/alanna"},"imageURL":"/img/alanna.jpg","key":"alanna"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"tshirt2024","title":"New T Shirt Fundraiser!","authors":["alanna"],"tags":["fundraiser"],"image":"./tshirt2024.jpeg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Fundraiser for Boba","permalink":"/blog/boba"},"nextItem":{"title":"A Big Announcement","permalink":"/blog/announce"}},"content":"Introducing Our New Rescue T-Shirts!\\n\\nWe are beyond excited to announce the launch of our new t-shirts featuring our adorable brand-new logo! \u{1F389} These shirts are not only cute and comfy but also a wonderful way to show your support for our small animal rescue as we relaunch. Every purchase goes directly toward helping us care for our rescue animals, provide medical attention, food, and all the love they need. \u{1F439}\u{1F430}\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![New Helping All Little Things Logo Tee](tshirt2024.jpeg)\\n\\nOur t-shirts come in a variety of colors, so you can choose your favorite and wear it proudly knowing that you\u2019re making a real difference in the lives of the animals we save. Whether you\u2019re a long-time supporter or new to our community, this is a fun way to help while spreading awareness for our cause. Plus, who wouldn\u2019t want to rock our adorable new logo? \u{1F496}\\n\\nHelp us continue our mission of rescuing and caring for the tiniest, most vulnerable animals by grabbing a shirt today. Thank you for being part of this amazing community\u2014we couldn\u2019t do this without you! \u{1F31F}\\n\\nHead over to the shop now and pick yours up before they\u2019re gone! \u{1F6CD}\uFE0F\u{1F455}\\n\\nhttps://www.customink.com/fundraising/haltrescuestartup\\n\\n<SupportUs />"},{"id":"announce","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/announce","source":"@site/blog/2024-09-07-announcement/2024-09-07-announcement.md","title":"A Big Announcement","description":"We have exciting news to share! Helping All Little Pipsqueaks is evolving and will be going back to our original name, Helping All Little Things \u2014 an independent rescue dedicated to small animals and sanctuary care. While we are branching out on our own, we want to reassure you that nothing has changed in our friendship with The Pipsqueakery. We\u2019re still great friends and will continue to collaborate! We are so grateful that Alex and the rest of the Pipsqueakery helped us get through these past tumultuous 4 years. Without them, we never would have survived as a rescue.","date":"2024-09-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"News","permalink":"/blog/tags/news","description":"News and announcements"}],"readingTime":1.27,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Alanna Burke","title":"Board Member, Foster Parent, Volunteer","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/alanna"},"imageURL":"/img/alanna.jpg","key":"alanna"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"announce","title":"A Big Announcement","authors":["alanna"],"tags":["news"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"New T Shirt Fundraiser!","permalink":"/blog/tshirt2024"}},"content":"We have exciting news to share! Helping All Little Pipsqueaks is evolving and will be going back to our original name, Helping All Little Things \u2014 an independent rescue dedicated to small animals and sanctuary care. While we are branching out on our own, we want to reassure you that nothing has changed in our friendship with The Pipsqueakery. We\u2019re still great friends and will continue to collaborate! We are so grateful that Alex and the rest of the Pipsqueakery helped us get through these past tumultuous 4 years. Without them, we never would have survived as a rescue.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThis transition allows us to focus more closely on what we\u2019re most passionate about: providing specialized care to small animals and offering sanctuary care to those in need. Meanwhile, The Pipsqueakery will continue their outstanding work with wildlife and domestic rescue, a mission we fully support and admire.\\n\\nWe\u2019re proud to announce that Helping All Little Things is officially established as a 501(c)(3) organization in New Hampshire and we will continue to do rescue work along the East Coast.\\n\\nYou can still find us on the same social media platforms, and we hope you\u2019ll continue to follow us and support our work as we embark on this new chapter.\\n\\nThank you for being a part of our journey \u2014 we couldn\u2019t do it without you, and we\u2019re excited for all the great things to come!\\n\\n### Follow us on:\\n\\n[Facebook](https://www.facebook.com/HALTRescue/)    \\n[Twitter](https://twitter.com/HALT__rescue)    \\n[Instagram](https://www.instagram.com/helpingalllittlethings/)    \\n[Mastodon](https://mstdn.social/@haltrescue)    \\n\\n<SupportUs />"}]}}')}}]);